window.harnessChunks["rows-752ce92ed0c2cff4.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T19:05:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/romiluz13/cc10x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cc10x</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/romiluz13/cc10x/tree/36c7b06a7c846d18cfbf33969ee0a7a2483985af\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 36c7b06a7c846d18cfbf33969ee0a7a2483985af\">36c7b06 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement, debug, plan, review or execute a targeted coding/QA task through a specialized Claude model agent with tool-feedback iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code behavior, test failures, integration surprises and findings discovered during the assigned phase. - Decisive decision or feedback right: worker model selects investigative/implementation steps, reads tool outputs, edits code where authorized and returns contract-structured results. - Decision owner: dispatched Claude model agent such as component-builder, bug-investigator or QA-executor, according to task type. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role prompts, agent-common and domain skills, Claude Agent tool, workflow phase artifact, handoff contract checks. - Closure path: router&#39;s phase scope → model agent tools/result interpretation → implementation/evidence → structured report → router records outcome or returns remediation. - Boundary reachability: official plugin install documents main router + built-in agents, and default task graph calls Claude Agent primitive; separate Task tools are optional with supported artifact-only mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: absent the specialized model, prompts and Python guards cannot choose substantive source edits or judge task evidence. - Evidence: router, builder workflow, README. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for documented/installed role loop; live Claude coding run not reproduced. - Caveats: external Claude Code provider/Agent primitive required for isolated cells; inline fallback does not prove independent role execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party regulation of interaction-driven interference between distinct active S1 cells identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: reviewer and failure-hunter may independently find issues, but they are read-only and their result aggregation is not an observed inter-agent resource collision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none specific to countering S1 peer interference. - Decision owner: no S2 owner established; task phase graph and conflicts in evidence are resolved by router current-control S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: blockedBy task dependencies, serial phase dispatch, parallel read-only review/hunt, artifact scope by workflow UUID and router result aggregation. - Closure path: no demonstrated peer interference → local coordination correction → changed affected S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 mode claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallel agents, role delegation, task dependency edges and validation sequencing are not enough without a concrete S2 variety witness. - Evidence: router, BUILD graph. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: router can handle conflicting audit opinions as current workflow control, but no distinct operational peer-contention loop has been proven.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: control the entire current coding workflow across specialized worker, audit, verification, remediation and memory steps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: contradictory evidence, incomplete phases, failed tests, growing scope, stale state, blocked permissions and budgeted remediation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: router model interprets user intent, selects workflow/complexity, authors dependent role graph, validates reports, decides remediation escalation versus pause, and re-enters phases until verified. - Decision owner: first-party instructed main-session Claude router agent, not state-persistence Python hooks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cc10x-router skill, UUID-scoped artifact/events, contract envelope validation, phaseexitgate, task tool or artifact-only graph, remediation history and stop rules. - Closure path: whole workflow state/agent findings → model router decides next phase or remediation → dispatch and persisted results → updated state and verifier feedback → next model decision. - Boundary reachability: plugin entry router is installed/activated for ordinary code requests and maintains authority in supported Agent dispatch mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic guards cannot choose the substantive phase topology, scope negotiation or remedy; those rights belong to router model. - Whole-system current view: router reconstructs all active phase state, task blockers, results and artifact history within the current workflow UUID and validates graph completion. - Current-control decision scope: role assignments, phase gates, escalation from trivial/full graph, remediation/re-review chain, accepted user clarifications and stop/continue. - Evidence: router, BUILD reference, state persistence. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for model ownership and standard instructions. - Caveats: enforced prompt process depends on compliance by external Claude model, and human approval/permission input is not a separate S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation and planning success claims with fresh or read-only model evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: review anchoring, hallucinated findings, green tests that prove little, unverified user flows and premature phase completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent read-only integration-verifier executes tests, independently checks code/line evidence and returns PASS/FAIL to router; PLAN uses a fresh anti-anchored plan-gap-reviewer. - Decision owner: separately dispatched Claude model agents under verifier/reviewer prompts; router receives verdict and controls remediation, rather than self-auditing builder claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated Agent dispatch, verifier read-only tools, per-scenario evidence contract, test honesty sweeps, fresh reviewer forbidden to load planner memory, phase exit gate and remedial re-review. - Closure path: builder/plan output → independently grounded reviewer/verifier evidence → blocking verdict → router creates REM-FIX/re-review and re-verification; only checked PASS advances. - Boundary reachability: full BUILD, trivial BUILD verifier, DEBUG and PLAN/QA graphs ship mandatory review/verification roles in supported subagent mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: another model agent explicitly reopens code, re-runs tests and adjudicates findings; deterministic gate and router relay rather than make the independent audit judgment. - Claim being audited: builder task goal/implementation correctness, source findings and phase exit readiness; PLAN mode challenges execution-plan completeness. - Ordinary reporting path: builder/reviewer structured handoffs and workflow artifact state into the router. - Complementary access path: separate read-only verifier&#39;s own direct code/test/tool observations and fresh plan reviewer&#39;s original request/plan/source investigation. - Independence boundary: verifier has distinct Agent session/tool access and must treat prior claims as UNVERIFIED; fresh plan reviewer excludes prior planner rationale and memory. - Who acts on findings: router blocks phase advance and schedules bounded remediation or pauses for user, then invokes new verifier. - Evidence: verifier, fresh plan reviewer, router. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for standard independent model review-and-return path; live provider audit accuracy not claimed. - Caveats: supported inline fallback loses independent subagent session; no S3 A inference from inline mode alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate outward-facing prospective organizational adaptation cycle established beyond current workflow memory/replay. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task failure and compaction are managed in present workflow; future external capability shifts not monitored in an evidenced closed path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party strategic future-option decision returned into present operating capability. - Decision owner: none for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reusable memory patterns, failure history, research agent, skills and session persistence. - Closure path: no established external horizon → future option → adopted change to current system method. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 mode claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: saving lessons and researching inside a task improves context but does not constitute a distinct outside-and-then intelligence loop. - Evidence: router, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user can request roadmap research as ordinary workflow work without creating S4 organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate identity/policy decision loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user permission gates, scope choices, review confidence and workflow opt-out are per-task operating constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party identity-level authority or subsequent governance return. - Decision owner: none for S5; Claude user permissions and project standards govern individual tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AskUserQuestion, user plan approval, permission settings, safety instructions and no-destructive-action rules. - Closure path: no identity-level proposal → legitimate authority → ultimate-policy return to agent operating mode. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 mode claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approvals for edits, branch finishing, risk and plans do not govern organizational identity/ultimate policy. - Evidence: router, BUILD workflow. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human managers of actual projects may supply independent policy, outside this packaged plugin&#39;s recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/94438b773ef572a514b6a8b6068ef1a160dbae11/assessments/cc/cc10x.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 94438b773ef572a514b6a8b6068ef1a160dbae11\">94438b7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T22:49:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope-java\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentScope Java 2.0</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope-java/tree/3d1b998d7c1c0aa6d9697a85b59c205a4c7c2a0f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3d1b998d7c1c0aa6d9697a85b59c205a4c7c2a0f\">3d1b998 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed reasoning and tool action for supplied tasks, including delegated independent subtasks in team mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous inputs, tool results/failures, context size, state recovery and diverse task subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agent chooses reasoning, next tool or teammate task action on feedback. - Decision owner: model-backed ReAct/HarnessAgent and configured team worker/lead agent roles. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Toolkit, model/permission middleware, AgentStateStore, workspace/sandbox, tool transport and invocation limits. - Closure path: prompt/state → model choice → tool call → tool result/event → subsequent model decision or task completion. - Boundary reachability: ReActAgent is the packaged core and HarnessAgent.Builder creates it with installed middleware and model/tool registries. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model makes semantic operational selections, while Java classes enforce schema/permission and handle state/transport. - Evidence: ReActAgent.java, HarnessAgent.java, architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for documented installed call path. - Caveats: hosted models, tools, data and application goals are caller-provided.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: mitigate competing work claims and contradictory or dependent teammate progress within one active team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple operational members may claim the same task, act before predecessors finish or report incompatible conclusions. - Distinct S1 units: multiple team-worker/lead model-backed HarnessAgent instances identified by team+member and operating on independent tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing task ownership and blockedBy dependencies; team lead prompt also requires resolution of disagreements among teammate results. - Attenuating coordination relation: a shared versioned task board implements exclusive compare-and-swap claims/assignments and dependency blocking; model lead sees team reports and can resolve disagreements/reassign work. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflicts return as tool errors; successful assignment or completion wakes the affected member and injects the task/result into its next reasoning turn. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: unlike a generic mailbox or task fan-out, task-ownership CAS and dependency checks prevent structurally specified competing operations and the team lead is explicitly prompted to reconcile conflicting teammate conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task allocation/reassignment or reconcile disagreements; prevent double claims and notify the next legitimate worker. - Decision owner: lead/worker model agents for allocation and response to feedback; LocalTeamClient deterministically rejects invalid claims. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamClient, CAS store, task owner/blockedBy fields, TeamTool, TeamsMiddleware wakeups/inbox. - Closure path: attempted claim/assignment → conflict/dependency check → changed board or denial → model/worker wakes, selects eligible work or lead reallocates it. - Boundary reachability: HarnessAgent.Builder registers TeamsMiddleware and TeamTool when teamsModeClient/context are supplied; TeamsMiddleware feeds board and reconciliation instructions to live model reasoning, not only tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantics of allocation/reconciliation belong to team agent choices; CAS is a necessary integrity enforcer but not the owner of team task discretion. - Evidence: LocalTeamClient.java, TeamTask.java, TeamsMiddleware.java, TeamTool.java. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, conditional on team mode and genuine multiple members. - Caveats: the CAS subpath is deterministic; A relies on the wired model lead/worker feedback decisions in actual team operation, not CAS in isolation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: control present team-wide commitments, task ownership and completion across multiple active workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a lead losing track of allocated work, failed tasks, idle team members and incomplete team commitments. - Whole-system current view: TeamsMiddleware.renderBoardDigest supplies the team lead with every task, state, owner, settled result, and the set of tasks it still owns; refreshed at each turn. - Current-control decision scope: lead model may create/assign/reassign tasks, spawn/shutdown members, reject/approve plans and complete a team only when the board is settled; worker actions are role-clipped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model lead interprets board and member feedback, decides which current work to assign, rescue or close. - Decision owner: lead model-backed agent in configured team mode, not the deterministic board or external user. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-scoped TeamTool, board persistence, wakeups, CAS and terminal-state checks. - Closure path: whole-board digest/member failure notice → lead decides intervention → TeamTool updates assignment/team/membership → changed worker tasks and later digest. - Boundary reachability: source builder wires team middleware and tools into the actual model call, and live onReasoning injects refreshed whole-team board into the lead&#39;s system message. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model is instructed and equipped to reason over entire board and select interventions; runtime enforces ownership and delivery but has no equivalent semantic resource/commitment judgment without the lead. - Evidence: TeamsMiddleware.java (renderBoardDigest, renderTeamSection), TeamTool.java, HarnessAgent.java (team mode wiring). - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for wired conditional mode. - Caveats: team governance is scoped to one run/team, not all externally deployed AgentScope users or cloud admin service.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate sufficiently independent inspection of ordinary team reports is established by the inspected runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: team worker errors, tool violations and misleading reports can be observed but only via ordinary logs, tests, task results and permission checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model lead receives worker reports through the same team task board; no separate independent operational-reality access/judgment/return exists in the standard team runtime. - Decision owner: regular team lead and configured verification hooks; not an independent audit unit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTraceMiddleware, permission system, tests, task state and logs. - Closure path: results and tool denials reach ordinary operating agents; no independent audit findings flow to team-wide corrective authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a reviewer subagent can be configured, but the generic declaration and agent trace alone cannot establish an independent complementary access path. - Evidence: TeamsMiddleware.java, AgentTraceMiddleware.java, subagent.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: independently built application-specific auditors remain possible outside the credited mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally oriented prospective option-development loop linked to adaptation of present team capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new user requests and accumulated skill usage change task execution and skill hygiene, not a modeled future environment or strategic response. - Decisive decision or feedback right: optional agent may propose/edit skills; optional human/local gate promotes them; periodic curator archives stale skills and optionally dry-runs merges. - Decision owner: agent for task-local skill drafting, operator for promotion, deterministic curator schedule for hygiene; none demonstrably owns S4 prospective adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SkillManageTool, promotion filters/gates, SkillCuratorMiddleware, memory maintenance and usage counters. - Closure path: approved skills can affect later agent capabilities, but the required outside/future distinction and prospective strategic alternatives/feedback are not evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: self-learning and durable skill changes are not sufficient for outside-and-then S4; human approval is not automatically S4 parent authority. - Evidence: skill.md, SkillCuratorMiddleware.java. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: caller-specific future intelligence built via middleware is outside the library&#39;s default function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installed ultimate-policy/identity deliberation-and-return for the assessed agent/team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, team role scope, plan-mode approval and workspace policies limit current work but do not arbitrate mission or ultimate values. - Decisive decision or feedback right: developer/operator sets tool permissions and team roles; the model receives role clipping and task/plan verdicts, not authority over identity-level policy. - Decision owner: user/configuration and application administrators outside a first-party constitutional return path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission middleware, TeamTool roles, plan approval, skill promotion gates. - Closure path: tool consent or plan verdict affects task execution, but there is no first-party identity question → legitimate authority → policy ratification → subsequent operation circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent&#39;s role or prompt cannot itself prove ultimate-policy authority; human task approvals do not become S5 parent governance. - Evidence: TeamTool.java, permission-system.md, skill.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a higher-level installed application may build governance; not credited to this standard Java harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4e6ba041fd81345e79cfe7f292c3f45b4666a0be/assessments/ag/agentscope-java-2-0.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4e6ba041fd81345e79cfe7f292c3f45b4666a0be\">4e6ba04 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T10:57:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mikeyobrien/ralph-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ralph Orchestrator</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mikeyobrien/ralph-orchestrator/tree/b5789dfbb25bd4aa65ce565c756d95acc8c42f76\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b5789dfbb25bd4aa65ce565c756d95acc8c42f76\">b5789df &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce working code, review or researched task artifacts through model-directed tool-use iterations against a real project environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing source/tool outputs, failing tests, partial code, unavailable backend responses and unresolved task requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the active backend agent selects investigation, edits, tests and event/continuation response, within the supplied role and user task. - Decision owner: model-backed agent session invoked in the supported Ralph loop, not the deterministic EventLoop dispatcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: backend adapters, prompt/hats, event bus, tasks/memory/scratchpad, persisted iteration state and cost/runtime/failure guardrails. - Closure path: user objective → Ralph invokes model backend with current context → model executes tools and emits state/event → Ralph stores outcome and selects subsequent iteration/hat → subsequent model action or evidence-backed completion. - Boundary reachability: ralph run with a configured supported backend is the public CLI path, not a development-only demo; code-assist is a bundled preset. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model decision-making leaves an event dispatcher and durable state, not the task-specific source-changing choices. - Evidence: backend execution/iteration, event/hat dispatch, Builder&#39;s actual TDD/tool duty. - Basis: structural shipped execution path plus explicit role prescriptions and unit/E2E fixture coverage. - Confidence: high for available autonomous S1 path, without claiming a paid live model run in this review. - Caveats: Ralph depends on externally installed agent backends; hard runtime limits and fixed role prompts constrain but do not themselves own task action selection.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: reconcile conflicting outputs of distinct concurrently operating worktree-loop S1 cells before they can become one coherent repository state in the selected auto-merge mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently completed branches can modify overlapping files or introduce mutually incompatible behavior; unsynchronized merges can break the shared working tree. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing model-backed Ralph loops in distinct Git worktrees, each contributing a task branch rather than only successive hats of a single loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two independent loop branches may edit the same file or cause a test failure when combined, so blindly admitting both would corrupt or destabilize the shared project. - Attenuating coordination relation: completed worktree loops submit to the durable merge queue; selected merge-loop mode uses a model-driven Branch Merger/Conflict Resolver and verification stages against the shared target branch. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: merge success makes the reconciled output available on main for later work; conflict/failing-test outputs lead the model to repair or leave an explicit needs-review state for the next operational decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path explicitly detects and resolves incompatible outputs of distinct operating loops at their common repository boundary, not merely delivering the next task event. - Decisive decision or feedback right: on a substantive conflict, the merge-loop model chooses how to preserve both branch intents and repair failing merged behavior; queue locks only transport and gate this discretion. - Decision owner: dedicated merge-loop model agent in the first-party preset; Git/file-lock state and test runner are supporting deterministic mechanisms. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree isolation, MergeQueue durable states, CLI subprocess spawning, Git merge/test invocations, conflict events and failure/needs-review escalation. - Closure path: independent loop outputs → queued branch → merge-loop conflict evidence/diff → model selects reconciliation/repair → changed main branch and verified outcome (or explicit unresolved state) → subsequent loops use new codebase. - Boundary reachability: parallel worktrees are supported; enabling shipped features.automerge actually enqueues and starts the bundled merge-loop preset after completion. The default manual-merge mode is not claimed to have autonomous S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the queue serializes transport but cannot semantically reconcile a C++/Rust/source conflict or choose correct combined behavior; a configured model agent does that in the positive mode. - Evidence: worktree completion queue, merge queue states and locks, merge-loop spawn, resolver/merger/tester roles, parallel and auto-merge defaults. - Basis: explicit configuration, positive source-level feedback path, and merge-queue/loop tests; no live merge conflict generated in this assessment. - Confidence: medium-high for selected supported coordination path; real conflict-resolution quality remains unmeasured. - Caveats: automerge is disabled by default and conflict resolution depends on the external model backend. In a solo or manual-merge-only deployment there is no positive S2 at the declared multi-loop relation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party model/parent controller with a whole-system current operating view and discretionary authority over all live loop commitments/resources was established for the assessed task/parallel-loop boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cost exhaustion, runtime failure, stale iterations, merge backlog and human-requested correction, mostly handled as local stop/retry/queue mechanics. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured budgets, maximum iterations, scheduler/role selection and operator interventions; these are preset/deployment policy or task-local operational choices, not an evidenced whole-system S3 judgment. - Decision owner: developer/operator for limits and selected task mode; backend agents for their own task work. No qualifying first-party S3 owner demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LoopState, TerminationReason, per-loop RObot status, merge queue, TUI/web status and runtime config. - Closure path: local failure/cost signals stop or retry a loop; there is no established decision from a total current operational view into revised cross-loop commitments or priorities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: stopping at a configured limit or displaying multiple loops does not transfer current-system resource discretion to an autonomous agent; merge reconciliation is S2, not whole-system S3. - Evidence: termination and state, configuration of limits and parallel mode, operator interface. - Basis: bounded structural negative after active role, loop, merge and operator path review. - Confidence: medium; whole-system control was not inferred from the product&#39;s “orchestrator” label. - Caveats: an external human project manager may provide S3 at a higher recursion, but their wider organization is not packaged as Ralph&#39;s first-party S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge an implementer&#39;s reported success with a fresh model-backed inspection of actual changed code, tests and failure paths, returning an independent correct/reject judgment to the implementation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false-positive builder completion, hidden regression, failed requirement/edge case and claims based only on self-reported test success. - Claim being audited: Builder&#39;s review.ready assertion that its task-specific code increment and verification satisfy acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: Builder emits review.ready after code/test work with task identity and claimed result. - Complementary access path: bundled code-assist Critic is a different hat/backend invocation, told to inspect actual changed source, rerun verification and an adversarial failure scenario instead of trusting Builder. The separate review-only preset provides an additional independently selectable direct-code inspection mode. - Independence boundary: role-separated Critic has independent review instructions, fresh turn context and an explicit pass/reject decision; no claim of external organizational independence or a separately audited provider model. - Who acts on findings: review.rejected activates Builder for repair; review.passed permits Finalizer to assess whole-task completion; subsequent task state reflects rejection/repair rather than accepting Builder&#39;s assertion automatically. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Critic model decides whether code evidence supports pass or requires rejection/correction. - Decision owner: Critic&#39;s separate model-backed role, not deterministic event routing or the Builder that authored the increment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review.ready event, hat subscriptions/publications, task IDs, required review event, backend execution and task/verification persistence. - Closure path: Builder source changes and review.ready → Critic independently inspects code/tests → review.rejected or review.passed → Builder repairs or Finalizer reviews completion → subsequent work acts on auditor judgment. - Boundary reachability: first-party published code-assist preset directly wires Builder/Critic/Finalizer and passes these events through ralph run, with no custom external audit plugin required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Critic&#39;s adverse code/test diagnosis is model discretion; the event bus only delivers its choice. Ordinary self-tests alone would not meet the complementary independence test. - Evidence: Critic, role separation, actual runtime/failure-path check and rejection, hat event routing, separate adversarial review preset. - Basis: explicitly shipped separate-agent review instructions and structurally closed pass/reject feedback. - Confidence: medium-high for selected mode; actual auditor independence/accuracy was not measured with live backend invocations. - Caveats: separate role invocation is narrower than external auditor independence; solo/preset-without-Critic runs do not acquire S3 from ordinary tests. This verdict is restricted to supported code-assist Critic mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped loop was established that senses externally/future-relevant changes, evaluates prospective options for Ralph&#39;s own operating capability and closes a strategic adjustment into current whole-system control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing coding-task details, research questions, locally measured improvement experiments and memory of task errors, rather than a sustained whole-system environmental-future strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agent/Strategist may plan a project experiment or choose an implementation adjustment; operator chooses new goals/backends/presets. - Decision owner: task agent for bounded research/optimization operations; user/developer for Ralph&#39;s model/capability policy. No separate first-party S4 right established at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: research and autoresearch task presets, memories, experiment logs, benchmark/rubric loops, configurable backend/hats. - Closure path: experimentation can improve the user&#39;s target artifact inside the active S1 task, but no evidenced future-environment model returns a capability/governance decision to a Ralph-level S3 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent-driven experiment planning is not automatically S4; its object is usually an operational deliverable, with no separate S3–S4 long-horizon conversation. - Evidence: autoresearch experiment preset, research-only preset, core configuration. - Basis: structural negative with explicit consideration of shipped positive experiment modes. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a human can employ Ralph to study future technologies, but such use does not by itself install an S4 for the harness at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/policy decision channel with legitimate governing authority and return to the running harness was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operator prompt changes, content/approval bounds, safe defaults, per-task human help, backend configuration and resource ceilings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/developer selects the task, configuration and permissions; the model can ask for task clarification but no identity-level matter is routed to a governing parent S5 by first-party protocol. - Decision owner: external user/deployment policy, not an autonomous Ralph S5 or evidenced operationally closed parent S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: guards, configurations, RObot human.interact, Telegram commands, stop/restart, role prompts, Git safety/merge defaults. - Closure path: human guidance changes a task iteration or deployment config, but no identity/ultimate-policy proposal → legitimate parent ruling → returned durable policy decision is defined for this system-in-focus. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approvals and human messages regulate immediate tasks; static instructions are not themselves identity policy deliberation or S5 closure. - Evidence: human interaction/Telegram scope, loop event/termination policy, configurable support modes. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the maintainers&#39; development governance or a deployment owner&#39;s wider organization may supply higher-recursion S5, but that is not the selected packaged task/loop boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/88b0c10833fb3161c4ab5c521bfb7f878088ab1a/assessments/ra/ralph-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 88b0c10833fb3161c4ab5c521bfb7f878088ab1a\">88b0c10 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T00:19:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nodecode-ai/nodecode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nodecode</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nodecode-ai/nodecode/tree/314476ddee650954f32695500be308276243f28b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 314476ddee650954f32695500be308276243f28b\">314476d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed task transformation through Lisp, shell and installed capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task/environment changes, tool failure, context limits and background work completing later. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next eval/action or answer based on accumulated observations. - Decision owner: The configured task agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent-loop, provider adapters, durable session facts, eval dispatch, background registry, context/recall and mutation persistence. - Closure path: execute-tool-calls appends actual results; agent-loop recurs with updated context, reading background exits before final answers when necessary. - Boundary reachability: Public installed runtime supplies the agent-loop and eval tool directly; model APIs provide inference rather than an imported agent harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model actor there is persistence/enforcement but no equivalent task-dependent action selection. - Evidence: src/engine/turn.lisp; src/engine/exec.lisp; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for shipped ownership. - Caveats: No live provider, task-quality or sandbox test was run. The repository explicitly states that eval is not a security sandbox.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference and destructive convergence among parallel task agents sharing one directory/image/artifact. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate approaches, concurrent artifact replacement, peer function redefinition and oscillating adoption of unverified results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select/revise a distinct approach, decide evidence-based adoption while preserving variation, and decide whether a candidate warrants promotion after rereading the shared best. - Decision owner: Team nodes mutually adjusting their work through the first-party agent protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Atomic slot claims, private work-K directories, LOG/DISCONFIRMED/ADOPTED, verified scores, best.lock and kernel fork-name fencing. - Closure path: Nodes reread peers&#39; approaches/results, change a conflicting or dominated strategy, reproduce candidate evidence and conditionally replace best under the lock; later nodes observe the new state. - Boundary reachability: Installed pinned team cell constructs and submits these prompts to each minted full-tool session via start-nodes/node-prompt. No developer-supplied coordination agent is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Locks enforce ordering, while model nodes choose the substantive approach and adoption response. Removing their judgment leaves serialization without mutual strategy adjustment. - Distinct S1 units: Separately minted team-node sessions with task execution and local work areas. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Shared approach/artifact/image collisions and repeatedly replacing a peer&#39;s stronger result without reproduction. - Attenuating coordination relation: Explicit approach differentiation, periodic peer-evidence rereads, validated adoption with retained variation, and conditional locked promotion. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Changed strategies and shared best/evidence influence subsequent node actions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The shipped protocol explicitly names cross-node collisions/convergence and prescribes agent-chosen corrective adjustment, not merely message transport. - Evidence: team/cell.lisp prompt/start-nodes; team/README.md; src/organism/fork.lisp fork-fence. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for the optional team mode. - Caveats: Board messaging or fork plurality alone would not establish S2. Coordination is behavioral rather than a security isolation guarantee.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Current regulation of the standing team&#39;s commitments and continuing resource use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Several active/idle/spent workers continuing an obsolete commitment or consuming budget under stale instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Redirect the standing team&#39;s work, stop the team or submit a new current commitment using its current collective state. - Decision owner: Legitimate operator of the local session/standing team. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-node usage folding, team roster/status, budget-hook, /team control, put-input steering and replacement of spent seats. - Closure path: /team reads current team state; /team TEXT reaches current nodes as a steer or follow-up and seats fresh nodes where spent nodes cannot continue; /team stop cancels the team. - Boundary reachability: These are installed native slash commands on the operator&#39;s session, with node sessions explicitly refused the right to seat another standing team. This is collective current control, not a generic single-action approval. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Node agents own local work; the operator supplies standing-team current commitments and intervention. Automatic budget enforcement/reseating does not select the operator&#39;s decision. - Whole-system current view: The standing-team roster, node activity/remaining spend and shared findings/artifact at the assessed team mode, exposed by result/result-text and /team. - Current-control decision scope: Team-wide work redirection, continuation/reseating and collective stop. - Evidence: team/cell.lisp node-spend/node-left, seat, put-input, run-slash, result/result-text; team/README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, explicitly limited to the standing-team mode. - Caveats: No autonomous S3 manager is claimed. An uninstantiated generic eval capable of management is not credited. Operator intervention over one ordinary tool call would be insufficient.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Post-turn challenge to operational claims and user-visible changes through separate verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A successful-looking task answer or write receipt despite an incorrect/unverified resulting state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reconstruct the intended invariant, choose narrow direct checks and adjudicate pass/fail/blocked, then repair within bounds or return actionable findings. - Decision owner: A separately instantiated experience reflection agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frame observer, frozen parent-turn anchor, dedicated child session, verification sightings, safe-tool fence, bounded repair budget and durable recap/attention delivery. - Closure path: Reflector reads actual results/durable facts, records evidence and verdict; a narrow supported repair is reverified, otherwise a next action/blocked finding returns to the originating session or operator. - Boundary reachability: The pinned experience cell&#39;s installed observer and worker invoke reflect-turn/fork-reflection after eligible task turns, using the bundled verification prompt and available safe verbs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The worker schedules reflection and stores its findings, but the separate agent chooses evidence and judges the invariant. Ordinary producer completion and deterministic receipt checks do not make that judgment. - Claim being audited: That user-visible code/config/data/live-surface changes and operational claims actually satisfy their intended invariant. - Ordinary reporting path: Original task agent answer, transcript and tool/write receipts. - Complementary access path: Separate reflector verification using existing safe verbs to read back actual result state and durable facts; its prompt explicitly rejects a write receipt as sufficient proof. - Independence boundary: Separate post-turn session/role and verification mandate, capable of outcome readback and challenge rather than repeating the original answer. It inherits context and may use the same model; independence is functional and limited, not a security separation. - Who acts on findings: Reflector for one bounded allowed repair; otherwise originating task/current operator via recap and attention request. - Evidence: experience/reflect.lisp reflection-prompt/fork-reflection; experience/cell.lisp installed observer/worker and repair setting. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for this optional mode. - Caveats: Cached inherited history can bias the reflector. Goal judges, ordinary runtime tests, candidate races and external PR triage are not separately counted as audit witnesses.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Outside-and-then intelligence at the declared installed-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible shipped regulatory paths were checked below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No material function-specific closure established. - Decision owner: No credited first-party owner for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mutable definitions, configuration and operator controls support operation without establishing this function. - Closure path: No qualifying shipped closure for this function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent access to eval does not by itself establish the separate organizational decision. - Evidence: src/organism/evolve.lisp; src/organism/knowledge.lisp; src/organism/update.lisp; experience/reflect.lisp; cyber-security/README.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the inspected distribution. - Caveats: Downstream deployments can add organizational behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Policy and identity at the declared installed-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible shipped regulatory paths were checked below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No material function-specific closure established. - Decision owner: No credited first-party owner for this function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mutable definitions, configuration and operator controls support operation without establishing this function. - Closure path: No qualifying shipped closure for this function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent access to eval does not by itself establish the separate organizational decision. - Evidence: src/organism/evolve.lisp; src/organism/profile.lisp; src/gateway/control.lisp; src/engine/turn.lisp; docs/CELLS.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the inspected distribution. - Caveats: Downstream deployments can add organizational behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b06d8ca3f59d03800d9ccb093505bd110b515243/assessments/no/nodecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b06d8ca3f59d03800d9ccb093505bd110b515243\">b06d8ca &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T17:04:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lanl/ursa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">URSA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lanl/ursa/tree/470b841d83e88457d5677fc824aea32e2d5a5e22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 470b841d83e88457d5677fc824aea32e2d5a5e22\">470b841 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: URSA-configured model agents autonomously investigate, plan, write or execute scientific work, calling tools and revising from live results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended research/engineering questions, uncertain search data and computational errors, incomplete or failing tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-driven ExecutionAgent chooses tool calls and continuation; named participating agents produce task-relevant results from their individual workspaces and tool feedback. - Decision owner: First-party URSA model/tool agent graph; configured backend model supplies inference rather than a separately authored external research harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph execution/checkpoint wrappers, named agent configuration, workspace, session state, tool registry, sandbox/safety checks, provider invocation and optional RAG/MCP support. - Closure path: prompt/task → model decides code/tool action → first-party tool node executes → workspace/tool observation returned → next model decision / final task result. - Boundary reachability: Installed ursa runtime registers and invokes named first-party agents; dashboard registry builds ExecutionAgent/other shipped agents; Agent Team and Symposium use these installed classes for their members and PI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent&#39;s configured LLM picks concrete operations based on observed tool state, rather than only executing a fixed static plan. Shell tools/search models remain dependencies. - Evidence: src/ursa/agents/base.py; src/ursa/agents/executionagent.py; src/ursa/cli/runtime.py; src/ursadashboard/registry.py. - Basis: Explicit first-party installed model/tool loop; structural source, not a live provider receipt. - Confidence: High for reachable code path. - Caveats: Requires model/API and actual scientific tools/data for practical work; no task success/performance measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate conflicting concurrent writable output ownership by multiple independent research S1 agents in the shipped optional Symposium mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simultaneously executing researchers writing common filenames, scripts, reports and intermediate scientific artefacts could overwrite/confound each other&#39;s work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The first-party Symposium constructor assigns each configured participant its own child workspace &lt;symposiumworkspace&gt;/&lt;membername&gt; before concurrent invocation, rather than allowing default shared-writable member workspace. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party workspace-topology constructor under configured member identity; no autonomous model-owned inter-S1 arbitration claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BaseEnvironment.memberworkspace, member factory, named/group state, initial asyncio.gather and revision participant workspaces. - Closure path: Distinct S1 member identity → per-member child work path selected → member&#39;s subsequent tool/code operations run against its own default workspace → independent results returned to Symposium → next participant revision again in member workspace. - Boundary reachability: A supported agentsymposium launch via dashboard environment worker instantiates this first-party default; direct AgentSymposiumEnvironment API and packaged sample configuration also exercise it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The concrete coordination choice is built into first-party topology; an agent is not shown dynamically choosing anti-collision strategy. This is C, not A, and not generic message transport. - Distinct S1 units: At least two separately instantiated configured Symposium member agents/nested teams with independent research outputs. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent conflicting writes or result overwrites if distinct S1 participants share the same default working directory and filenames. - Attenuating coordination relation: Per-member child workspaces localize each participant&#39;s default write target; review temporarily switches to parent workspace only for a declared inspection phase. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Path context passed to each configured member&#39;s subsequent run directs its tools/output to its own child path; revised artefacts remain scoped and visible for later review. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The assessed relation changes conflicting writable resource ownership for parallel separate researchers, not merely who receives a prompt or when the next step runs. - Evidence: src/ursa/environments/base.py; src/ursa/environments/agentsymposium.py; src/ursadashboard/environmentworkermain.py; examples/environments/agentsymposium.yaml. - Basis: Structural first-party conflict attenuation in shipped optional operating mode. - Confidence: Medium for bounded default-workspace collision reduction. - Caveats: Not a security sandbox or enforced cross-member write prohibition; arbitrary filesystem tools could cross directories. Shared-workspace Agent Team mode does not inherit this claim.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: The model-driven Agent Team PI regulates current team-level research commitments and correction among currently available member agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Divergent partial research results, missed commitments, mismatched specialist expertise and need to reassign/follow up while satisfying the shared current goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: From a whole-team roster, overall goal and returned member outputs, PI chooses current member assignment, follow-up work or direct synthesis to address gaps. - Decision owner: Configured first-party model-driven PI agent with delegated member tools, not dashboard background scheduler or static script. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team config, named agent/member registry, first-party dynamic delegateto tools, per-member call/result events, optional nested teams and provider model. - Closure path: overall goal + roster → PI discretionary member commitment choice → first-party delegation tool → member tool/model execution → outcome and evidence returned to PI → PI can choose follow-up/revised assignment and final integration. - Boundary reachability: The shipped dashboard worker starts AgentTeamEnvironment; buildpi actually attaches delegation tools to ExecutionAgent, and ainvoke calls PI with whole-team instructions. These are installed, configured first-party behaviors, not external supervisor CLI assumptions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model PI selects and revises team current commitments conditional on returned results. The observation is bounded to task/team scope, not lasting across independent projects or resource budgets; deterministic dispatch executes its choices. - Whole-system current view: Declared task and full configured team roster, plus member results and evidence of previous dispatches in PI conversation context. - Current-control decision scope: Which present team agent tackles a research subproblem, whether to ask a member for correction/additional work, and when the integrated task can close. - Evidence: src/ursa/environments/agentteam.py; src/ursa/environments/base.py; src/ursadashboard/environmentworkermain.py; examples/environments/agentteam.yaml. - Basis: Explicit reachable opt-in whole-team model decision right with action/return. - Confidence: Medium: prompt-mediated scheduling and follow-up, not guaranteed intervention in every execution or durable institution-wide commitments. - Caveats: A default single Chat/ExecutionAgent has no proven whole-team S3. Static PlanningExecutor step sequencing alone would be insufficient. No preemption of in-flight member calls or sophisticated resource/budget bargaining is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A separate Symposium participant challenges another agent&#39;s research/output claims by independently examining cross-member work, then returns evidence-backed criticism for revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unverified self-reported research correctness, erroneous code or weak evidence that can survive an author-only status and ordinary completion check. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer model judges validity, strength, reproducibility and needed corrections of other participants&#39; submitted writeups/artefacts; author model then decides revised work informed by review. - Decision owner: Separately instantiated configured reviewer participant model(s) in the opt-in Agent Symposium environment, for claims made by different producer agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer membership default reviewer: true, actual complete writeup collection, review-only instruction, parent-workspace inspection, independent participant invocation, first-party mapping of review results to each next revision prompt. - Closure path: ordinary member output/writeup → separate reviewer gets other participants&#39; output + parent child-artifact access → reviewer develops challenges/evidence → critique per review round → first-party revision prompts to original writers → changed subsequent member submissions → organizer synthesis. - Boundary reachability: Dashboard environment worker actually creates AgentSymposium from packaged class/config; ainvoke runs initial participant work, explicitly chooses review-enabled members, invokes them on cross-member materials, collects critiques, and executes further revision rounds. The example config has multiple members, including an independent research path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Review judgment is discretionary to a separate model-driven member; the deterministic round scheduler and presenter only transmit findings. A mere reviewwork call on ExecutionAgent or same-channel DeepReview role swap was not used for the positive claim. - Claim being audited: Another participant&#39;s scientific/code writeup is adequate, evidence-based and reproducible. - Ordinary reporting path: Original writer&#39;s answer, file outputs and internal completion review to Symposium. - Complementary access path: Peer model gets all actual participant writeups (including other workers&#39;) and, during review, parent-workspace view of other participants&#39; child files; can inspect or run referenced code to assess reproducibility. - Independence boundary: Distinct configured agent instances, participant identities and task workspaces, reviewed by a different author/participant. Not a separate model vendor by requirement, not a cryptographic or physical write-protect boundary: reviewers may inspect their own outputs, the same provider/model may be reused and no-edit is instruction-only. - Who acts on findings: The Symposium constructor distributes review text to every original participant via their revision prompt; those model agents revise their own submitted writeups, then the organizer receives updated outputs. - Evidence: src/ursa/environments/agentsymposium.py; src/ursa/environments/base.py; src/ursa/environments/config.py; src/ursadashboard/environmentworkermain.py; examples/environments/agentsymposium.yaml; src/ursa/agents/executionagent.py; src/ursa/agents/deepreviewagent.py. - Basis: Source-attested cross-actor complementary read/review/revise feedback loop in installed optional mode. - Confidence: Medium for first-party prompt-enforced agent independence and corrective return. Low for any stronger claim of immutable artifacts, clean-room evidence, adversarial non-collusion or robust gate. - Caveats: No empirical reviewer accuracy, run-time contamination controls, code immutability or guaranteed finding acceptance. The single-agent fail-open completion reviewer is expressly excluded.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No substantiated future/environment-oriented adaptation that changes URSA&#39;s organizational capability and returns it to subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-level research uncertainty, new web/RAG results, changing plan/task hypotheses; those are operational problem-solving rather than future harness adaptability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party model, constructor or eligible parent mode shown selecting longer-horizon change to URSA&#39;s organizational capabilities in response to external trends. - Decision owner: Research-task models for evolving research answers; package maintainers/operator for non-runtime manual changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planning reflection, DeepReview iterative solution refinement, optimisation agents and session/checkpoint persistence. - Closure path: These can refine current research output, but no future/environment sensing → proposed change of agent/workflow capability → adopted operational configuration → later run is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scientific hypothesis evolution, optimization and internal benchmark tasks are not inherently VSM S4 adaptation of the URSA organization. - Evidence: src/ursa/agents/planningagent.py; src/ursa/agents/deepreviewagent.py; src/ursa/agents/optimizationagent.py; src/ursa/cli/runtime.py; src/ursa/environments/agentteam.py. - Basis: Bounded absence after checking opt-in team/symposium and adjacent adaptive task sources. - Confidence: Medium-high for current supported operational scope. - Caveats: If an actually installed cross-run capability change loop is evidenced at same pin, re-assess rather than extrapolate from naming.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No supported first-party ultimate identity/policy determination returned to govern the scientific-task organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Configured workspace scope, selected models, shell safety checks, dashboards/users and team config; these are policy inputs and task gates, not evidenced ultimate identity settlement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/developer specifies local settings and research goals; no installed model/constructor or eligible parent authority resolves an ultimate organization-level policy/identity conflict and returns binding policy to activity. - Decision owner: Task owner chooses settings; no function-specific S5 owner evidenced in the shipped assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Security checks, validation, model/tool configuration, named agent/team member metadata and optional dashboard auth. - Closure path: No first-party identity/policy issue → recognized ultimate authority → returned authoritative decision → governed next operation was identified. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Named groups, access credentials, human research approvals and selection of provider/agent parameters do not automatically establish S5. - Evidence: src/ursa/environments/config.py; src/ursa/cli/runtime.py; src/ursa/security.py; src/ursadashboard/security.py; src/ursa/environments/agentteam.py. - Basis: Bounded negative review of shipped policy/configuration/operational paths. - Confidence: Medium-high within declared task-level recursion. - Caveats: Los Alamos organizational policy, repository project maintainership, or external credentials are distinct authorities not imported into this task harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/797d290cfc83c2142a6a924a43070d8f66cbc6c0/assessments/ursa.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 797d290cfc83c2142a6a924a43070d8f66cbc6c0\">797d290 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T22:55:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/iOfficeAI/AionUi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AionUi</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/iOfficeAI/AionUi/tree/6744099b279b991c17e31c243f0920477bd31cb6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6744099b279b991c17e31c243f0920477bd31cb6\">6744099 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. AionUi ships a built-in autonomous agent engine and Team Mode runs autonomous Leader/Teammate agents through supported backends. These agents perform the operational file/tool/task work rather than merely exposing constructor slots.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. Team Mode closes a concrete coordination loop across distinct S1s: tasks carry owners and explicit dependency/blocker relations; teammates execute in parallel; and the autonomous Leader decomposes and assigns work while the runtime exposes blocked/queued/running state that changes subsequent team behavior. This regulates duplicate, premature and mutually dependent work rather than merely transporting messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The autonomous Leader has a whole-team current view and authority to assign, track and aggregate teammate work, while Team Mode exposes whole-run/per-slot state and dynamic member control. That establishes first-party agent-owned regulation over current team commitments and progress.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Per-agent approvals, status and ordinary result aggregation are part of the normal execution/control path; no materially independent complementary audit channel with distinct evidence access and corrective closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. External search/tools may support current tasks, but no distinct outside-looking prospective organizational adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Human permission dialogs and deployment policy constrain actions, but routine execution approval is not an organizational identity or ultimate-policy tension-and-resolution loop. The historical P therefore does not survive the function-first S5 test.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd/assessments/aionui.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9b431dfc4e818567d073e9a545bf346497b4addd\">9b431df &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T23:12:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Eigent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/eigent-ai/eigent/tree/6c49956b2aa878c08bee5edcf8a1eb63122c2cd9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6c49956b2aa878c08bee5edcf8a1eb63122c2cd9\">6c49956 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Workforce agents autonomously execute assigned tool/model work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: A. The task agent produces an explicit dependency graph and runnable work is published only after dependencies are satisfied; the model-driven workforce path selects assignees and reacts to task state. This closes dependency/ordering interference across autonomous S1s rather than merely routing messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The model-driven coordinator has a workforce-wide view and authority over assignment, retries/replanning and current task progression. These are current whole-team regulation decisions owned by an autonomous first-party agent path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. No materially independent complementary audit channel with distinct evidence access and corrective closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Planning and replanning address current task execution; no separate prospective environment-facing organizational adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Human permissions/configuration constrain execution but do not establish identity/ultimate-policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af/assessments/eigent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aa0f4fc244d04c276678f55ed941376422a344af\">aa0f4fc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T14:26:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/awslabs/cli-agent-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CLI Agent Orchestrator</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/awslabs/cli-agent-orchestrator/tree/ffa9fc742c7d47ce038d3d951ccc5527ba0c0218\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ffa9fc742c7d47ce038d3d951ccc5527ba0c0218\">ffa9fc7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Multiple model-directed worker sessions implement assigned coding/review tasks and return artifacts/findings to the supervisor through the supported CAO runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, changing code, individual task failures and defects, separate worker provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CAO-started worker agents decide how to perform the delegated task within their advertised developer/reviewer role; the supervisor allocates tasks separately. - Decision owner: Shipped first-party configured agent profiles running model-driven sessions through supported external CLI providers; external provider reasoning details are not credited as first-party code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Profile roles, terminal/provid­er adapter, model process, CAO MCP messages and terminal session manager. - Closure path: Supervisor task/message → first-party-created worker CLI context → worker produces code/review findings → CAO handoff output or inbox callback → supervisor sees completion and selects follow-up. - Boundary reachability: The documented install/launch path exposes built-in codesupervisor; its MCP server can launch developer/reviewer sessions from installed first-party profiles (README.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Work and review choices are generated by actual delegated agent roles, not a deterministic tmux replay; no claim is made about external provider-native internal planning. - Evidence: README.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/developer.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/reviewer.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent damaging shared-source interference among separately acting coding workers when the supported opt-in worktree isolation mode is used. - Distinct S1 units: Independently launched developer/worker terminal sessions via the first-party assign/handoff MCP tools (src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py). - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent spawned workers share a Git checkout by default and can race/overwrite one another&#39;s edits or create merge conflicts; first-party worktree service explicitly identifies these exact hazards (src/cliagentorchestrator/services/worktreeservice.py). - Attenuating coordination relation: Supervisor&#39;s opt-in worktree parameter causes CAO to create distinct branch/checkouts for individual child sessions, separating conflicting write contexts; worker results return to the supervisor for explicit integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Worktree-provisioned workers execute in separated checkouts, return task outcome through handoff or inbox callback; supervisor then selects further assignments/repair or integration rather than allowing concurrent edits to the original shared checkout (src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py). - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Evidence ties per-worker isolation directly to an explicitly documented concrete inter-worker file race/overwrite hazard, not to ordinary message delivery or terminal creation alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping mutations and checkout interference between active coding units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The choice to request isolated worktree mode belongs to supervisor/constructor configuration; CAO creates a fresh branch/worktree and enforces the isolated execution context. - Decision owner: First-party optional isolation constructor with developer/supervisor-provided enablement; no autonomous S2-level admission or merge-convergence policy is shown. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-worker server-generated terminal ID, Git worktree creation, per-terminal checkout path, cleanup, MCP assign/handoff callback. - Closure path: Explicit collision risk → assign/handoff with useworktree=True → distinct Git branches and isolated next S1 edits → result delivered to supervisor → subsequent unit work/integration decisions. - Boundary reachability: useworktree is a shipped argument on both first-party MCP tools and forwarded to the local terminal service; createworktree makes an actual branch/checkout (src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/services/worktreeservice.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Contention-specific relation is built by CAO; the optional activation owner is the supervisor but no independent agent S2 conflict-resolution policy is presumed. - Evidence: src/cliagentorchestrator/services/worktreeservice.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/services/worktreeservice.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Positive restricted to explicitly enabled per-worker worktree mode; default shared directory does not receive this S2 closure. No automatic conflict-free merge or code acceptance is claimed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: First-party model-driven codesupervisor governs commitments and rework across its active developer and reviewer worker units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfinished assignments, failed/defective code, availability and outputs of launched agents, shared artifact paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supervisor chooses which worker gets each task, when findings trigger rework and when review is satisfactory. - Decision owner: Bundled codesupervisor model role, not the tmux scheduler, status endpoint or underlying provider&#39;s hidden native agent hierarchy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CAO assign/handoff, result callbacks, inbox delivery/status, supervisor file/task directory rules. - Whole-system current view: Within the expressly bounded supervisor team, supervisor tracks assigned tasks, known code artifacts, callbacks and reviewer findings, not all sessions/fleets of external agents. - Current-control decision scope: Developer/reviewer assignment, repair/retry commitments and task convergence at the supervisor-team recursion. - Closure path: Subordinate outcomes and reviewer feedback → supervisor checks team current work and chooses assignment/retry/approval → new worker work and returned completion. - Boundary reachability: cao install codesupervisor and cao launch --agents codesupervisor are documented; its first-party role/instructions require task assignment, progress tracking, rework on failed review, and delegate through wired MCP tools (README.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervisor decides commitments based on returned worker/reviewer observations; tool transport/terminal service only enforces dispatch and delivery. - Evidence: src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/utils/orchestration.py. - Basis: explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A distinct code-review worker audits produced source/code artifacts and sends independent problem findings back into the supervisor&#39;s repair/acceptance loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Code quality, correctness, missing test coverage, security vulnerabilities in developer-produced work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Dedicated reviewer model makes its own source-inspection judgment; supervisor decides whether findings require developer repair. - Decision owner: Separate built-in reviewer model session for audit judgment; supervisor owns follow-up commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party reviewer role/tool-access limits, shared artifact paths, CAO handoff/assign and return message mechanism. - Claim being audited: Developer completion/quality claims about newly produced project files. - Ordinary reporting path: Developer sends work artifacts/status to the CAO supervisor. - Complementary access path: A separately instantiated read-oriented reviewer receives source paths and inspects code independently of the developer&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: Separate worker terminal/profile, read-oriented reviewer tools and independently model-produced findings; it is neither the developer&#39;s own loop nor a deterministic assertion of completion. - Who acts on findings: Supervisor relays defects to developer, repeats review and decides when satisfied. - Closure path: Developer artifact → separate reviewer source examination → findings to supervisor → subsequent developer correction/re-review before finishing. - Boundary reachability: Built-in supervisor explicitly requires a code-review worker after development and repair loops; the shipped reviewer profile has a review-only role and sends findings back over the first-party MCP channel (src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/reviewer.md; docs/tool-restrictions.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct model reviewer produces content-specific defect judgments; model independence is role/process-level, not proof of different provider weights or guaranteed statistical independence. - Evidence: src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/reviewer.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/mcpserver/server.py. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Positive only in the bundled developer+reviewer supervisor mode, not every launch. Reviewer is independently running but no claim is made of organizational independence outside this task-level CAO team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Optional learning/outcome/retrospector/instruction-promotion path might adapt future agents, but its external-prospective S4 function and decisive owner remain unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Recurring historical workflow failures and future task differences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Retrospector stores lessons; separate operator must explicitly apply promoted lessons into agent profiles, but it is unproven whether this constitutes full outside-and-then intelligence rather than retrospective process optimization. - Decision owner: Mixed autonomous retrospector and operator for promotion; no complete S4 autonomous or first-party parent mode established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Outcome records, memoryrecall, retrospector profile, opt-in learning flags, cao memory promote --apply. - External distinction: Work outcomes can carry evidence of changing task environments, but the explicit external opportunity/threat prospective appraisal is not established. - Future / prospective distinction: Promoted lessons affect future new sessions, unlike an ordinary retry. - Adaptation option generated: Retrospector may distill up to three reusable lessons; human-triggered promotion writes them to future profiles. - Path back into current capability / S3: Profile learned patterns apply on later sessions; first-party prospective external intelligence judgment/authority boundary still under review. - Closure path: Reported outcome → retrospector lesson → user promotion → future profile instructions, without an established independent outside-and-then appraisal. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatic memory capture and config editing do not by themselves establish the organizational S4 function. - Evidence: docs/self-learning.md; docs/self-learning.md; docs/self-learning.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/services/promotionservice.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: First-party tool roles and operator approval policy restrict action, but ultimate project identity/organizational policy authority return path remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool permissions, agent identities and allowed delegation, workflow safety/custody and operator oversight. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/deployer selects launch permissions and approvals; roles bound available MCP/CLI actions. - Decision owner: Operator for safety and profile policy; model supervisor chooses within constraints. No S5-specific parent closure established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role-based allowedTools, profile schema and permission checks, workflow plan approval gate with opt-in setting and persistent approved plan ID. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Accepted tool/role policy of the CAO session and approved workflow intent. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Not credited; operator can approve specific actions, but project-wide identity/policy decision loop has not been established. - Return-to-operation path: Profile/tool restrictions affect what worker may call; workflow approval gates permit/deny runs. These are enforcement/approval paths, not proven ultimate-policy closure. - Closure path: Agent attempts tools → permission/approval checks → allowed/blocked execution; unresolved higher-level policy decision ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Read-only reviewer role and guarded workflow approval do not turn static constraints or generic human approval into S5. - Evidence: docs/tool-restrictions.md; src/cliagentorchestrator/services/approvalgate.py; src/cliagentorchestrator/agentstore/codesupervisor.md. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1dc2d2e352399be6e8e0ae673bd65d038049a708/assessments/cli-agent-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1dc2d2e352399be6e8e0ae673bd65d038049a708\">1dc2d2e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T20:13:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Deep Agents</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents/tree/f86b4e9abef7620b63a7258bc9fab0ccd83de8a4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f86b4e9abef7620b63a7258bc9fab0ccd83de8a4\">f86b4e9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the primary and delegated agents autonomously perform model/tool work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: concurrent async tasks, isolation and lifecycle APIs establish multiple S1 units but no specific first-party inter-S1 interference/oscillation plus attenuation relation. Start/update/cancel/list are hierarchical current-control rights, not mutual-adjustment evidence by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the main agent has a live view of its tracked background commitments and model-facing authority to start, inspect, redirect/interupt, cancel and list those commitments. The decisive intervention is chosen by the autonomous main agent; SDK/LangGraph machinery transports and enforces it. This closes a current whole-subagent-set regulation loop without a developer having to supply another control actor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: a separate rubric grader challenges a candidate completion and can independently gather read-only evidence through the first-party dcode wiring. needsrevision findings are returned into the primary loop and cause further work. This is materially complementary access plus autonomous audit judgment and feedback closure, rather than ordinary inline QA.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: rubric iteration, memory, skills and task updates concern present execution/quality. No separate prospective environment-facing loop that changes future organizational capability/strategy was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: HITL/interrupton, rubrics and runtime options can gate operational actions or define acceptance criteria, but they do not establish identity/ultimate-policy closure. Generic human interruption is therefore not S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c/assessments/deepagents.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c\">3c76cda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T20:13:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QwenLM/qwen-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Qwen Code</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QwenLM/qwen-code/tree/3ac6fe65e81c84897c02d22abd3dc91d1e567d5f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3ac6fe65e81c84897c02d22abd3dc91d1e567d5f\">3ac6fe6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: coding agents autonomously perform tool-using work inside their assigned scope.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: multiple S1 teammates share task ownership and messages. Atomic claiming prevents duplicate task ownership, priority delivery changes subsequent teammate behavior, and bounded pending-message queues attenuate a concrete flood/interference mode. Agents choose task/message actions while TeamManager provides deterministic enforcement and feedback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the team leader is an autonomous current-control actor over the team. It can create/direct teammates and invoke a leader-only shutdown that removes a teammate from participation and releases its task commitment; TeamManager supplies the whole-team state/lifecycle substrate and closes the intervention. Separately, Qwen Code&#39;s interactive approval mode can require the parent user to allow/deny these ask-classified team-control actions before execution, establishing a distinct parent-governed S3 mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no distinct complementary audit actor/path with materially different access to operational reality and findings returned into current control was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: no separate outside-and-future adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: approval modes and team-action permissions govern operational interventions. They do not establish a first-party identity/ultimate-policy question and legitimate ultimate-authority loop, so the previous generic S5=P interpretation is removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c/assessments/qwen-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c\">3c76cda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/agent-framework\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft Agent Framework</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-16\">2026-09-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/agent-framework/tree/1cd06c5a2058a172eebadf5d9d7c3fa45c519520\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1cd06c5a2058a172eebadf5d9d7c3fa45c519520\">1cd06c5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: specialist agents autonomously perform bounded tool/model work and return operational outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the Magentic manager actively detects loop/stall/no-progress conditions and changes team execution to restore convergence. This is agent-owned regulation of instability among operational units, not static graph topology.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the default Magentic manager owns a whole-team current-state ledger of facts/plan/progress, chooses who acts next, and has reset/replan authority over current commitments, establishing an autonomous S3 mode. The first-party plan-review option establishes a second parent-governed mode over the same S3 function: before a plan executes, a human sees the current progress ledger and proposed whole-team plan and may approve it or require revision; revision is returned to the manager, which replans before work continues. This is materially different from participant tool-call approval because it controls the team&#39;s current plan itself.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the same manager that regulates current work observes progress; no organizationally distinct complementary audit channel with independent access/authority was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: replanning responds to current execution failure. The reviewed implementation does not establish a separate prospective external-environment intelligence function that adapts future system capability/strategy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: goals, participants, instructions and orchestration policy remain application-defined. Human signoff on a current Magentic plan is S3 current-control authority, not by itself ultimate organizational identity or policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/microsoft-agent-framework.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/matiasrodlo/veritas\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Veritas</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/matiasrodlo/veritas/tree/f366432381834dee8ecb17bcbc2bad3e6232ff0b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f366432381834dee8ecb17bcbc2bad3e6232ff0b\">f366432 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: generate, debug, execute, compare, and refine experiment implementations to gather scientific evidence for the loaded research idea. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code/runtime failures, inadequate implementations, hyperparameter uncertainty, poor metrics, scaling choices, and uncertainty about which experimental change best tests the hypothesis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose candidate implementation changes and subsequent experiment actions in response to execution errors, journal history, metrics, and stage goals. - Decision owner: the model-driven tree-search research agent (ParallelAgent/candidate-generation path). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated interpreter, execution timeout/resource controls, journals/tree nodes, named metrics and provenance, multi-seed evaluation, artifacts/plots, search configuration, and checkpoints. - Closure path: generated code is executed; errors/metrics/artifacts return into the search journal and prompts; subsequent nodes implement revisions or alternative experiments until stage criteria/limits are reached. - Boundary reachability: the tree-search agent and interpreter are the core path invoked by launchscientistbfts.py; no external agent harness supplies the experiment loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic execution and metric parsing constrain/measure candidates, but the model-driven agent chooses substantive implementation and experiment changes. - Evidence: README search description, tree-search runner, AgentManager agent creation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parallel candidates/tree branches are alternative attempts inside the operational research process; their plurality is not itself evidence of S2.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific inter-S1 coordination loop is established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker isolation, process/resource limits, stage sequencing, shared journals, and candidate ranking prevent contamination or structure search but do not evidence a concrete interference/oscillation among distinct cooperating S1 units. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrent or branching experiment attempts are not automatically separate operational units requiring S2; they are candidate searches for one S1 research outcome. - Evidence: README execution/isolation description, experiment runner. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a future multi-researcher mode would require a distinct S1 mapping and explicit interference/attenuation witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain current evidence about the whole active research search/stage and decide completion, next-substage focus, and progression of the experiment program. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stagnating search, unmet stage success criteria, unresolved implementation issues, insufficient improvement, under-scaled experiments, and uncertainty about the most useful next search focus. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether the current stage/substage is complete and generate specific goals/focus for the next substage from current progress/issues/metrics. - Decision owner: AgentManager&#39;s model-driven feedback/evaluation path for discretionary completion/focus judgments, operating within deterministic minimum/max/adaptive-stopping constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: full stage journal, best-node selection, success-criteria checks, improvement counters, deterministic stopping rules, stage history, checkpoints, and stage lifecycle code. - Closure path: current journal metrics/issues/recent changes are summarized into the feedback model; the resulting completion decision or generated substage goals alter stage lifecycle and the prompt/goals used by the next research agent. - Boundary reachability: AgentManager is the standard controller instantiated by the main tree-search runner, and its feedback model/path is configured as part of normal experiment execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed timeouts/max-iterations and metric checks are enforcement. S3 credit comes from the additional model-driven current-control judgment that interprets current whole-stage evidence and creates the next research focus. - Whole-system current view: active stage journal, all attempts in that stage, good/buggy nodes, best metric/node, success-criteria progress, current issues/recent changes, stage history, and prior-stage best implementation when relevant. - Current-control decision scope: stage completion, continuation versus progression, and specific next-substage research goals/focus that determine subsequent resource/effort commitment within the run. - Evidence: AgentManager completion logic, next-substage goal generation, standard runner. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every AgentManager decision is autonomous: many limits/checks are deterministic. The positive state depends specifically on the model-driven completion and next-focus decisions wired into current operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material operationally closed complementary-audit loop is established in the reviewed full-run path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S3 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent audit right with corrective return is established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: metric provenance, multi-seed checks, plot/VLM checks, zero-data abort, and final automated paper review provide validation/review surfaces. - Closure path: the final review is written after the experiment search and paper generation, but the reviewed launcher does not feed its findings back into AgentManager, write-up, or experiments for corrective operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: performreview is a separate review action by name, but S3 requires complementary audit plus findings-to-control closure. Post-hoc persisted review without return is insufficient. - Evidence: launcher review path, README output including reviewtext.txt. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: internal VLM/metric validation is part of ordinary search acceptance/current control, while the separate final review lacks corrective return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: in the supported idea-generation mode, sense external scientific literature, reflect on novelty/feasibility, and produce a future experiment program that the standard research run can execute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk that a proposed research direction duplicates existing work, misses relevant prior findings, or lacks a feasible/meaningful experimental plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/refine/finalize the research proposal after incorporating Semantic Scholar retrieval into repeated idea reflection. - Decision owner: the model-driven idea-generation agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party Semantic Scholar tool, multi-round reflection history, prior-idea archive, structured FinalizeIdea schema, and instruction requiring at least one literature search before finalization. - Closure path: external literature results enter the reflection loop; the finalized idea includes related work, hypothesis, experiments, and risk factors; the resulting JSON is directly consumable by launchscientistbfts.py, where it determines subsequent staged experimental capability/work. - Boundary reachability: README documents performideationtempfree.py as the first-party way to create an ideas file from a short workshop brief; its output is the same schema consumed by the standard launcher. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the literature tool retrieves external distinctions, but the model-driven ideation loop owns proposal selection/refinement; the experiment launcher then operationalizes the returned option. - External distinction: retrieved Semantic Scholar prior work and related-work information outside the current Veritas run. - Future / prospective distinction: novelty, feasibility, and which hypothesis/experimental direction should be pursued in later stages. - Adaptation option generated: finalized research idea containing hypothesis, related work, experiment plan, and risks/limitations. - Path back into current capability / S3: the idea JSON becomes the launcher&#39;s task description; AgentManager derives stage work from its experiment plan and executes the resulting research program. - Evidence: idea-generation tool and required literature search, README documented idea generation, launcher idea loading. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the internal creativeresearch stage and reuse of previous experiment results are not the basis for S4. Credit depends on the distinct first-party external-literature → future idea → launched experiment closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is established at the declared research-run recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user/workshop idea, structured idea schema, stage goals, configuration, model choices, resource limits, and review/write-up settings constrain operation. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is shown reaching a legitimate authority and returning as a binding decision to subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: idea generation chooses the scientific direction to pursue, which is credited as S4; it does not evidence authority to redefine the system&#39;s ultimate identity/governance policy at runtime. - Evidence: idea schema and launcher, AgentManager stage contract. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human selection of the input idea/configuration is not automatically parent-governed S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/veritas.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The AI Scientist-v2</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist-v2/tree/96bd51617cfdbb494a9fc283af00fe090edfae48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 96bd51617cfdbb494a9fc283af00fe090edfae48\">96bd516 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform executable ML research experiments through model-driven proposal/code changes, execution and feedback-guided search. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation failures, changing metrics/plots, alternative model/data choices, debugging needs and experimental outcomes across tree-search nodes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete experiment/code changes and search-node actions based on current task description, parent node/history and execution feedback. - Decision owner: first-party parallel experiment/search agents instantiated by AgentManager. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: journals/nodes, experiment executor callbacks, workspace isolation/state, stage-specific task descriptions, search budgets and GPU/process management. - Closure path: research-stage goal/current node -&gt; agent proposes implementation/experiment -&gt; runtime executes it -&gt; metrics/errors/plots are recorded in journal state -&gt; agent/search policy chooses subsequent node/action. - Boundary reachability: the tree-search agents are created and run by the shipped AgentManager used directly by launchscientistbfts.py. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: schedulers/GPU management enforce execution resources, but the context-sensitive experimental action is selected by model-driven agents. - Evidence: README.md, agentmanager.py, parallelagent.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material agent-owned S2 coordination path is established among the parallel S1 search workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workers share finite execution resources and contribute nodes to experiment search, but no agent-owned interference/oscillation detector and attenuation relation is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel worker scheduling, GPU manager/allocation, journals and tree-search selection. - Closure path: resource/scheduling code assigns/releases execution capacity and search code selects nodes; this is deterministic orchestration rather than an S2 conflict-regulation conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worker plurality and GPU allocation are not promoted to S2 without an agent-owned response to a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: parallelagent.py, agentmanager.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-experiment current view and adaptively control stage completion, continuation and next experimental focus on behalf of the research run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or unstable implementations, insufficient dataset coverage, weak progress, missing stage criteria, changing best-node evidence and current experimental issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether the current sub-stage/stage is complete and generate measurable next sub-stage goals/focus from current experiment evidence; advance or continue the staged research program accordingly. - Decision owner: the model-driven feedback/control queries executed by the first-party AgentManager over its whole current stage/journal state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage/journal state, best-node selection, VLM plot feedback, structured completion function schemas, hard bounds/fallback rules, checkpointing and stage-transition machinery. - Closure path: current journals/best node/metrics/plot feedback -&gt; manager control query judges completion or identifies missing criteria -&gt; manager continues current stage or creates a new focused sub-stage/main stage -&gt; subsequent agents receive the revised stage goals and operate under them. - Boundary reachability: AgentManager is instantiated by the standard BFTS experiment path and its run() loop directly creates worker agents and applies the completion/focus decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed stage ordering and max-iteration bounds are deterministic support, but the materially adaptive completion/focus judgments are model queries that change subsequent whole-run experiment commitments. - Whole-system current view: current stage, all stage journals/history, best implementation, success counts/metrics, VLM feedback, tested datasets and current issues/progress at the declared experiment-run recursion. - Current-control decision scope: whether to continue/complete the current stage and what concrete goals/focus the next sub-stage should impose on all subsequent experiment workers. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, performexperimentsbftswithagentmanager.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary audit loop returning sufficiently independent findings into the S3 current-control path is established at the reviewed revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: VLM plot analysis, multi-seed evaluation and final paper review provide evaluation signals, but their roles do not establish a separate operational audit channel with corrective return to S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plot/VLM feedback, multi-seed evaluation, LLM text review and image/caption/reference review. - Closure path: VLM/metric feedback is consumed as part of ordinary manager evaluation; final paper reviews are written to files after write-up and are not returned into a correction/re-control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluator agents/models exist, but evaluation naming or separate inference alone is insufficient without a complementary independence-and-corrective-return closure. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, launchscientistbfts.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense external literature and prospective scientific opportunity, generate/refine a future research proposal, and return that option into current experiment execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: prior literature may make ideas non-novel; alternative hypotheses/experiments may differ in feasibility, impact and relation to existing work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose search queries, incorporate retrieved literature into reflection, and finalize a structured future proposal/hypothesis/experiment plan. - Decision owner: first-party ideation LLM agent using the shipped Semantic Scholar tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SemanticScholarSearchTool, action/tool protocol, reflection loop, structured FinalizeIdea schema and JSON handoff to launcher. - Closure path: workshop/topic description -&gt; ideation agent searches external literature -&gt; retrieved results alter reflection/proposal -&gt; agent finalizes structured idea -&gt; launchscientistbfts.py consumes that idea and executes the experiment program. - Boundary reachability: performideationtempfree.py is the documented first step for generating the JSON consumed by the standard experiment launcher. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the search tool supplies evidence, but the prospective research-option synthesis and finalization are agent decisions. - External distinction: Semantic Scholar literature and related-work evidence. - Future / prospective distinction: novelty, feasibility, risks and which hypothesis/experiments should be pursued next. - Adaptation option generated: structured research proposal containing hypothesis, related work, experiments and limitations. - Path back into current capability / S3: generated idea JSON becomes the task description for AgentManager and its experiment stages. - Evidence: performideationtempfree.py, README.md, launchscientistbfts.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workshop/topic descriptions, stage goals, risk-factor prompts, model choices and BFTS configuration constrain research, but they do not form an identity-level ultimate-authority loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workshop description, proposal schema, hard-coded main-stage goals, YAML search configuration and CLI arguments. - Closure path: configuration/prompt policy directly constrains the run; no identity/ultimate-policy issue is escalated to an authoritative actor and returned as governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent manager can adapt current experimental focus, but it is not given authority to redefine the system&#39;s identity or ultimate research policy. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, performideationtempfree.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/ai-scientist-v2.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The AI Scientist</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SakanaAI/AI-Scientist/tree/1de1dbc1f4ee2c5f61e9c94348d55eb51d7fa2eb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1de1dbc1f4ee2c5f61e9c94348d55eb51d7fa2eb\">1de1dbc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement and execute research experiments and produce the resulting scientific artifacts within a maintained experiment template. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code/runtime errors, differing experimental ideas, metric outcomes, plotting/write-up needs and project-file state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete experiment/code edits and subsequent actions based on the idea, baseline state and observed execution/results. - Decision owner: the active model-driven Aider coding/experiment agent used by the first-party experiment loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: copied per-idea workspace, baseline results, performexperiments, Aider file editing, process execution, notes/logs and bounded experiment flow. - Closure path: idea/baseline/workspace -&gt; agent edits experiment implementation -&gt; code executes -&gt; result/error evidence returns -&gt; agent chooses further experiment edits or completion. - Boundary reachability: the loop is invoked by the documented launchscientist.py entrypoint for every novel idea in a standard paper-generation run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Python launcher logic enforces sequencing, but the context-sensitive experiment/code choices are model-agent decisions. - Evidence: launchscientist.py, README.md, aiscientist/performexperiments.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple ideas may execute in parallel on different GPUs, but the workers are independent attempts; no concrete cross-S1 conflict/oscillation and attenuation relation is evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: multiprocessing queue, GPU assignment and independent per-idea workspaces. - Closure path: scheduling distributes independent jobs; it does not return a coordination decision to change behavior because of inter-S1 interference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: resource scheduling is deterministic runtime support and parallelism alone is not S2. - Evidence: launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no agent-owned whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failures/success at experiment/write-up/review stages change control flow, but stage ordering and stop/advance rules are fixed by the launcher. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-run current view and discretionary authority over current shared resources, commitments, priorities or constraints. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: launcher stage sequence, exceptions/return values, GPU/process setup, skip/improvement flags and fixed success gates. - Closure path: deterministic success/failure and CLI configuration drive stage transitions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: local agents decide experiments/write-up content, while whole-run control remains scripted. - Evidence: launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the produced research manuscript and, in the supported improvement mode, return findings into a separate corrective write-up path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported/unclear claims, presentation weaknesses, methodological weaknesses and other defects observable by a paper reviewer but not necessarily corrected during ordinary write-up. - Decisive decision or feedback right: produce a structured peer-review judgment/weakness set over the completed paper; those findings become explicit corrective input in improvement mode. - Decision owner: the first-party automated paper-review invocation, separate from the Aider writer/coder session being reviewed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PDF loading, review ensemble/reflections, persisted review.txt, performimprovement, PDF regeneration and second review. - Closure path: writer produces PDF -&gt; separate reviewer evaluates it -&gt; structured review/weaknesses -&gt; performimprovement(review, coder) returns findings to writer/coder -&gt; revised PDF -&gt; reviewer evaluates the revised artifact again. - Boundary reachability: --improvement is an explicit supported launcher mode using first-party review and improvement functions; it requires no external custom orchestration beyond normal model credentials. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer owns the semantic challenge judgment; the launcher only transfers review findings and enforces the second pass. - Claim being audited: that the research manuscript adequately presents/supports the generated scientific work at peer-review quality. - Ordinary reporting path: experiment notes/results -&gt; manuscript generation by the coding/writing agent. - Complementary access path: a separate review invocation reads the rendered paper and evaluates it with a review-specific prompt/ensemble rather than reusing the writer conversation. - Independence boundary: writer/coder and reviewer are separate model interactions/roles; review is generated after the artifact is materialized. - Who acts on findings: the Aider writer/coder through performimprovement, followed by manuscript regeneration. - Evidence: launchscientist.py, aiscientist/performreview.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: generate prospective research options, interrogate external literature for novelty and return novel ideas into current experimentation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: existing prior work may make a candidate idea non-novel; alternative ideas differ in feasibility/interestingness and should change the next experiment attempted. - Decisive decision or feedback right: propose/refine research ideas and, during novelty checking, choose literature queries and decide whether each idea is novel enough to enter the experiment set. - Decision owner: first-party idea-generation/novelty-checking model agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idea reflection archive, Semantic Scholar/OpenAlex search, structured idea JSON, novelty flag and launcher filtering to novelideas. - Closure path: template/task context -&gt; agent proposes/refines idea -&gt; external literature searches return evidence -&gt; novelty judgment marks idea -&gt; only novel ideas are dispatched into executable experiments. - Boundary reachability: idea generation and novelty checking are invoked by the standard launcher unless explicitly skipped; their JSON/novelty outputs directly gate experiment admission. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: search APIs provide external observations, but query selection, idea refinement and novelty judgment are model-agent decisions. - External distinction: retrieved titles/abstracts/metadata from Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a proposed idea is sufficiently novel/feasible/interesting to justify a future experiment. - Adaptation option generated: structured experiment idea with title, experiment outline and quality ratings. - Path back into current capability / S3: launcher filters ideas to novelideas and runs each accepted idea through doidea experiment/write-up execution. - Evidence: aiscientist/generateideas.py, launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: experiment templates, prompt/task descriptions, model/engine flags and safety/container advice constrain operation, but no identity-level issue is routed to an ultimate authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: template prompt.json, CLI options, maintained template structure and static safety guidance. - Closure path: configuration/prompt/template policy directly constrains work; no identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research agents can select ideas/experiments but do not own authority to redefine the system&#39;s organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md, launchscientist.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/ai-scientist.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:48:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nsidn98/LLaMAR\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LLaMAR</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nsidn98/LLaMAR/tree/d2b74cac79a0f070d87a150712f8e2ae174920be\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d2b74cac79a0f070d87a150712f8e2ae174920be\">d2b74ca &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute the embodied work required to complete the shared task through robot-specific actions in the environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: partial observations, object locations/states, navigation/manipulation requirements, action failures and changing environment state during a long-horizon episode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose each robot&#39;s next substantive environment action from the current observations/state and interpret returned success/failure when selecting what happens next. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven LLaMAR action/controller path over the embodied robot streams. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AI2-THOR/SAR environment adapters, feasible-action mapping, per-agent observations/states/action histories, task/subtask state and deterministic execution plumbing. - Closure path: team task/current plan -&gt; model selects an action for each robot -&gt; environment executes each embodied action -&gt; per-agent observation/state/success/failure returns -&gt; later model call selects changed robot actions -&gt; episode progresses. - Boundary reachability: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py and SAR/baselines/llamar.py are shipped runnable first-party episode entrypoints and directly execute the credited action/environment feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: simulator code transports actions and state, while the substantive next-action choices are model-generated from the live episode context. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py; SAR/baselines/llamar.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: next-action authority is centralized in the model call rather than separately modelled inside each physical robot, but the operational behavior is still first-party agent-owned at the assessed team boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete interference among simultaneously operating embodied robots sharing the same physical workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: robots blocking/bumping one another, congestion around shared objects/receptacles, one robot&#39;s manipulation/navigation preventing another robot from completing its assigned action, and unnecessary simultaneous movement into the same constrained area. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose joint next-step actions such as waiting, moving aside, closing an obstruction or delaying one robot so another robot can proceed without cross-agent interference. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven joint action/controller path using whole-team state and failure context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared observations/state, previous action/failure fields, combined task/memory state, explicit anti-blocking prompt constraints and per-robot joint action output. - Closure path: multiple robot operations interact in one room -&gt; execution exposes cross-agent blocking/failure -&gt; the next model call reasons about the interference -&gt; assigns compensating actions across the involved robots -&gt; later robot behavior changes and the blocked operation can proceed. - Boundary reachability: the standard LLaMAR actor prompt is called on every episode step from the runnable baseline and directly receives/returns the team state and per-agent joint actions used by env.step(). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the anti-interference choice is contextual model reasoning over current cross-agent state; deterministic code only executes the selected joint actions. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic task allocation is not the basis for S2; the positive state rests on the separately evidenced physical interference problem and feedback relation. - Distinct S1 units: the concurrently acting embodied robot streams (Alice, Bob and additional configured robots), each with its own observation/state/action/success history and physical action effect. - Inter-S1 disturbance: one robot can physically block/bump another or occupy/open an object/path such that another robot&#39;s navigation/manipulation fails or must wait. - Attenuating coordination relation: the joint controller sees all robots&#39; state/history and is explicitly instructed to avoid blocking/bumping; failure reasoning can prescribe wait/move-aside/clear-path behavior across the involved robots. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the model&#39;s coordinated per-robot action dictionary is passed through actionchecker and then to env.step(), changing the next embodied action of each robot. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the decisive witness is regulation of a concrete interaction disturbance among simultaneous embodied operations, not the existence of multiple robots, shared memory or a task decomposition graph.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain the whole team&#39;s current task commitments and intervene in the active multi-robot plan as completion/failure/environment information changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing team progress, completed versus still-open commitments, newly observed environment information and failures requiring the active plan or current robot allocation/actions to change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the current shared open-subtask plan for the team and, through the whole-team action/controller stage, determine which operational action each robot should take now under that plan. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven planner and whole-team action/controller path. - Whole-system current view: the planner receives the task, current observation from every configured robot, and the shared open/completed subtask state after each verifier step; the action/controller additionally receives every robot&#39;s state, previous action/failure, shared subtasks and combined memory. - Current-control decision scope: preserve or revise the team&#39;s active unfinished commitments and assign the next concrete actions/subtasks across the current robot population, including interventions after failures and current spatial conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared open/completed-subtask state, verifier updates, whole-team observation aggregation, combined memory, planner prompt, action/controller prompt and repeated plan-update loop. - Closure path: robot operations execute -&gt; team observations/actions and completion state update -&gt; verifier updates completed commitments -&gt; planner forms a revised whole-team open plan -&gt; action/controller maps that current plan/state to robot-specific next actions -&gt; changed operations execute and report back. - Boundary reachability: the planner/action stages are invoked in every standard runnable LLaMAR episode; no external orchestrator is required to form the whole-team view or return interventions into robot operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic choice of the current plan and per-robot intervention is model-driven; deterministic code stores plan/completion state and transports decisions into the environment. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py; SAR/baselines/llamar.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S3 credit is not based on the component name Planner; it rests on the whole-team current view plus returned decisions changing the active shared commitments and robot actions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: the verifier provides in-band completion reporting, but no materially independent complementary-audit function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect assumptions about whether a subtask has completed can be corrected before replanning, but the checked claim remains part of the ordinary task-progress loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor with a distinct authority/evidence channel is established; the verifier prompt only classifies completed subtasks for the same episode. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3; the verifier uses the ordinary model service/configuration and shared episode evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompt, all robot observations/states/previous actions, shared open/completed subtasks and combined memory. - Closure path: robot actions execute -&gt; ordinary episode observations feed verifier -&gt; completed-subtask list feeds planner -&gt; current plan changes; this is ordinary progress reporting/monitoring without an evidenced independent audit boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a semantic verifier exists, but verifier naming and a separate prompt-stage do not establish complementary independence. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; AI2Thor/envnew.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the verifier does influence subsequent planning; the missing requirement is organizational/complementary independence, not lack of feedback.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective capability-adaptation loop is established beyond current-episode planning/replanning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: LLaMAR adapts its current task plan to new observations and failures, but does not evidence an actor that senses an external future environment, generates capability-change options and installs a change for later operational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for prospective adaptation of the installed harness/robot organization across episodes. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current observation updates, planner replanning, memory and failure reasoning. - Closure path: environment change/failure -&gt; current episode replanning -&gt; changed immediate robot actions; no future-oriented capability option is implemented into later installed capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current replanning is S1/S3 regulation, not evidence of S4 merely because the plan changes. - Evidence: AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: long-horizon planning and replanning are substantial capabilities but remain inside the present episode boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity or ultimate-policy authority is established at the multi-robot episode boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task text, model/configuration parameters, prompt rules and simulator limits constrain behavior but do not constitute identity-level adjudication. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for defining or reconciling the team&#39;s ultimate identity/purpose/policy under competing S3/S4 demands. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-selected task/scene/configuration, static prompts and runtime constraints. - Closure path: external task/configuration enters directly as operating constraints; no identity-level issue -&gt; legitimate ultimate authority -&gt; policy decision -&gt; returned governance loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the models control current operational/coordination/management choices but do not choose the organization&#39;s ultimate identity or policy. - Evidence: README.md; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamar.py; AI2Thor/baselines/llamar/llamarutilsmultiagent.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static system prompts describing robot roles/constraints are not S5 by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a6735e44e0989bceeb433c4133d76671108cc563/assessments/llamar.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a6735e44e0989bceeb433c4133d76671108cc563\">a6735e4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T16:28:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DemonDamon/AgenticX\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgenticX</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DemonDamon/AgenticX/tree/582c9d00bfb23efe6f7e588c3a5edfce570729bf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 582c9d00bfb23efe6f7e588c3a5edfce570729bf\">582c9d0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended user tasks by choosing tools, using execution results, and modifying task artifacts through root/child provider-backed agent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new task details, tool errors, repository content, provider responses and subtask outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next model tool call, interpret feedback and continue/revise an execution plan. - Decision owner: configured model-backed AgentRuntime/ReAct root and spawned agents; neither the SDK Agent data model nor the event queue owns model reasoning. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider resolver, agent/tool loop, Studio session persistence, subagent context/tool permissions and callbacks. - Closure path: model chooses a tool, runtime executes it, the result enters the same agent&#39;s context, and later decisions change task output. - Boundary reachability: this path is shipped both through the agx/Studio local backend and embedded SDK; Near&#39;s default app starts this local runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent model makes contextual tool/continuation decisions; host code supplies guardrails and transport. - Evidence: AgentRuntime, ReActAgent, Studio server Meta-Agent setup. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: successful live provider/model execution and claimed production quality were not measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidence-backed distinct relation that autonomously attenuates an identified interference among simultaneously acting S1 units, beyond task partition and bounded queueing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential overlap of parallel workers, resource contention and conflicting changes would require interference-specific handling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent-owned cross-worker conflict detection, reconciliation or isolation decision shown as an operationally closed coordination loop for the declared Studio team. - Decision owner: none established at S2; the model planner assigns tasks and host TaskLock transports actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team task decomposition, worker assignments/dependencies, TaskLock action queue, group routing and shared context. - Closure path: delegations can complete and summaries return, but that is ordinary task routing; no distinct detected inter-S1 collision and corrective relation is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming a CoordinatorAgent or serializing actions does not establish function-specific S2 conflict attenuation. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model-backed child agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: same-file edits or contested resources are plausible, but the inspected supported team path does not independently establish a response to an observed conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: no qualifying inter-S1 relation established; task lists and a queue prevent generic scheduling races, not semantically competing work ownership. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: worker results and messages return to the manager/leader, but without a verified conflict-specific control decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: S2-specific ownership is not credited for generic dispatch or action queue operations. - Evidence: Team mode wiring and TaskLock, TaskLock, WorkforcePattern. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: developers may compose custom S2 adapters with the SDK; that is not automatically the shipped Studio default.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current root-session team by observing children and deciding when to spawn, communicate, retry or cancel during live task delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active child runs may stall/fail, saturate concurrency or CPU/memory, need new work or require intervention to meet the immediate user task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and invoke status query, spawn/cancel/retry/message subagent tools in light of reported current child state and goals. - Decision owner: configured root model-backed Meta-Agent within the supported Studio session, not the AgentTeamManager or the deterministic ResourceMonitor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTeamManager actual live status registry, timeouts, caps and resource admission checks; root Meta-Agent tool dispatcher; Studio event stream. - Closure path: root sees team status/tool results, chooses a current-team intervention, AgentTeamManager changes a child run/permission to launch, and updated state returns to the root&#39;s next model turn. - Boundary reachability: Studio builds the root Meta-Agent with visible team-management tools, and the attached AgentTeamManager executes them on real session children in the shipped local backend. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current worker-team decisions are controlled by the root model, while deterministic caps merely reject inadmissible actions. - Whole-system current view: querysubagentstatus and team status provide running/completed/failed child state and resource-monitor context for the session team. - Current-control decision scope: the current portfolio of active/retryable child agents in one root session, including admission and cessation; not Enterprise gateway quotas or an unrelated Studio user. - Evidence: team lifecycle/status/cancel, meta tool handler, resource monitor. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the model might choose not to use these tools; the claim is an available integrated operational loop, not a guaranteed policy of continuous proactive optimization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently controlled complementary reality-audit path with corrective control established for the current Studio session team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an executor can mistakenly report complete work while the real environment differs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor&#39;s direct-evidence veto/intervention against the team report established in the selected operating mode. - Decision owner: none established; SDK evaluation/LLM judge and Enterprise audit logging are separate or routine surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: traces, tool results, observability and optional evaluation framework. - Closure path: tests/evaluations can be explicitly launched, but a first-party independently owned standing complementary team audit with returned corrective control is not demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluation APIs, group worker results and log chains cannot substitute for a separate auditor owner. - Evidence: evaluation/observability overview, team results, Enterprise gateway separation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: externally composed independent reviewers are possible through the SDK, but their independence cannot be credited to the shipped session team.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: convert observations of changing external tool/provider/task conditions and user corrections into future reusable skill capability for subsequent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated external tool failures, newly learned environment conventions and multi-step strategies that existing installed skills do not yet encode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the background model-backed review agent decides whether and how to create/patch a reusable skill; under the normal supervised mode the Desktop operator separately decides whether to approve the proposed future capability. - Decision owner: opt-in skill-review agent in the direct-authorized mode (A), and legitimate Desktop-user approver for a separately supported queued-proposal mode (P). These modes are not simultaneous autonomous governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registered SessionReviewHook, actual tool observation records, signal/complexity gate, configured review model, skillmanage, skill policy scan, pending proposals and desktop approval endpoint. - Closure path: a completed tool-using session triggers a review of observed attempts/errors/user corrections; the model proposes a skill; in explicitly authorized direct mode it writes the skill for later sessions, while in default queue mode the Desktop user approves the same proposed document and installs it. Later model runs can discover/use the changed skill collection. - External distinction: observations concern user corrections and tool/provider outcomes from the task environment, not just the host runtime&#39;s own counters; the model interprets their reusable implications. - Future / prospective distinction: a post-session reviewer encodes a method for later task contexts rather than repairing only the completed current task, and the installed skill survives that session boundary. - Adaptation option generated: choose a new action-oriented skill or a patch to an existing skill, including the specific steps and guardrails for future comparable disturbances. - Path back into current capability / S3: after authorized direct installation or Desktop-user approval, the skill is added to the local installed registry so a later Meta-Agent/runtime session can discover and use the revised capability under its current task controls. - Boundary reachability: AgentRuntime registers SessionReviewHook; learning.reviewenabled is an active shipped configuration option, agentwritesrequireapproval distinguishes queue versus direct install, and Studio/Near expose proposal inspection/approve/reject routes. Both paths require configured model/provider access; neither is default-on just because observation is enabled. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer model selects substantive future reusable methods, not just a deterministic score; the operator retains the separate approval right in the supervised mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no closed ultimate-purpose or identity-policy governance path for the local Studio agent-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts about the team&#39;s permanent charter, legitimacy or non-negotiable identity beyond ordinary tasks/permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no runtime identity-level final authority and returned policy choice established in the selected local session hierarchy. - Decision owner: none established for this function; product maintainers, Enterprise administrators and local user are adjacent authorities for different matters. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: operator permissions, skill-approval gate, safety policies, user prompts, Enterprise Gateway policies and contributor governance. - Closure path: users permit/deny operations, administrators configure Gateway routing, and skill approvals modify future capabilities; these are operational/safety/adaptation choices rather than a reconstructed identity-level decision loop in local Studio. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a Meta-Agent CEO label does not create sovereign identity control, and separate enterprise governance does not transfer to deployed Studio S5. - Evidence: runtime/Enterprise boundaries, Studio tool gates, Meta-Agent identity prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no finding about how the open-source project or enterprise tenants should normatively govern themselves is made.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d83ab15f2643bc53884a269c9555beaea45091fb/assessments/ag/agenticx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d83ab15f2643bc53884a269c9555beaea45091fb\">d83ab15 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T17:45:27Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pipecat</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pipecat-ai/pipecat/tree/dbdf21a017f86624fb7768e35730417169524e0d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbdf21a017f86624fb7768e35730417169524e0d\">dbdf21a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform an environment-facing conversational/agent task by selecting model responses and model-callable tools from live input/context and incorporating tool/service outcomes into subsequent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user utterances, conversation state, tool needs/results, external-service responses, media events and task-specific runtime outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive next response/tool action in the shipped model/tool loop. - Decision owner: the configured LLM actor invoked through Pipecat&#39;s first-party LLM service/pipeline surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context aggregators; tool schemas/callbacks; transports; pipeline frames; jobs; timeouts; service adapters; tracing/metrics. - Closure path: user/environment input enters pipeline → Pipecat constructs model context/tools → model chooses response/tool action → runtime executes/delivers it → observations/results are returned into later context and operation. - Boundary reachability: this is Pipecat&#39;s ordinary supported runtime, not a repository-development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model decision actor leaves transport/execution machinery but removes the open-ended choice of conversational/task action. - Evidence: README.md; llmworker.py; openclaw-agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider implementation is external, but Pipecat deliberately places the model actor inside its supported operational feedback loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concurrent/interleaved output interference between distinct autonomous LLM workers during a live handoff and ensure that only the selected worker owns the next conversational operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the target worker can begin producing while the source worker still has output in flight, causing both workers&#39; outputs to arrive interleaved; overlapping activation would make ownership of the user-facing turn ambiguous. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide when to transfer conversational ownership from the current autonomous worker to another worker in response to the live conversation. - Decision owner: the currently active LLM worker in the first-party local-handoff mode, through the model-callable transfertoagent tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activateworker; source-pipeline drain; mutually exclusive activation/deactivation; shared worker registry/bus; tool result that suppresses another current LLM run during transfer. - Closure path: current LLM observes conversation → model selects transfertoagent → source worker drains outstanding output → runtime deactivates source and activates target without overlap → subsequent user-facing operation is produced by the selected target worker. - Boundary reachability: the behavior is implemented by first-party worker APIs and exercised by a runnable first-party multi-worker example whose documentation explicitly states that the LLM decides when to transfer. - Distinct S1 units: the active greeter/customer-service LLMWorker and the target support LLMWorker are separate model-driven operational workers sharing the conversation transport through the worker runtime. - Inter-S1 disturbance: without handoff drainage/exclusivity, the target worker can begin producing while the source worker still has output in flight, and both outputs can arrive interleaved. - Attenuating coordination relation: activateworker drains the source pipeline, deactivates it, then activates the selected target so the two workers do not simultaneously own user-facing output. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after the model-selected handoff, the target worker becomes active and receives/acts on subsequent conversation turns while the source is inactive. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism exists to attenuate a documented interference between two autonomous operational workers, and its result changes their subsequent operational participation; credit does not rely on WorkerBus messaging or delegation alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces safe switching, but the semantic choice of when/where to transfer is made by the active model from live conversational evidence. Removing that model decision leaves a callable handoff primitive, not the same coordination judgment. - Evidence: local-handoff-two-agents.py; worker.py; baseworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic WorkerBus messaging/job routing is not used as S2 evidence; credit depends on the concrete interleaving disturbance and the model-selected handoff closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current subordinate agent commitment while preserving a responsive voice/conversation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: long-running delegated work can be idle, active, obsolete after a user correction, redirected, cancelled, failed or completed while the conversational front end continues receiving new input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: from live subordinate-job state, choose whether to dispatch/update work, inspect current status or preemptively stop the active subordinate commitment. - Decision owner: the voice LLM in the first-party OpenClaw-agent composition; its context exposes sendtoagent, stopagent and agentstatus as model-callable tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ActiveJob; bus job requests/updates/responses; job-group cancellation; WorkerRunner; OpenClaw worker; developer messages that return job outcomes to the voice model. - Closure path: voice LLM receives current user input plus returned job state/outcome → model chooses direct response or a current-control tool → Pipecat dispatches/updates, reports or cancels the subordinate job → bus update/response changes ActiveJob and is returned to the voice loop → later current-control decisions use the changed state. - Boundary reachability: the complete path is a runnable first-party multi-worker example built on public first-party worker/job APIs. - Whole-system current view: at the selected voice-plus-agent recursion, the voice regulator tracks the only subordinate operational commitment (jobid, request, start time and later response/update state) while continuing its own user-facing work. - Current-control decision scope: it can create/change the live delegated commitment and preempt it; this is stronger than read-only status or runtime lifecycle cleanup. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the LLM itself selects the exposed dispatch/status/stop tools from current conversational evidence. Runtime code transports/enforces those choices; it does not make an equivalent semantic judgment when the model is removed. - Evidence: openclaw-agent/README.md; openclaw-agent.py; runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the OpenClaw backend decides whether a forwarded update starts a new internal turn or steers an existing one. S3 credit is not assigned to that backend decision; it is assigned to Pipecat&#39;s model-facing authority to observe, issue/update and cancel the current subordinate commitment at this recursion. Generic WorkerRunner end/cancel behavior alone would be constructor lifecycle support, not agent-owned S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit loop was established that independently inspects current operational claims and returns findings into the live operation/current-control loop for corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Pipecat can evaluate bot conversations and report assertion/judge failures, but those evaluation outcomes are separate test/eval results rather than a shipped production correction loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3 at the assessed runtime boundary. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scripted and simulated eval drivers; optional EvalJudge; expectation matching; pass/fail result objects; traces; ordinary worker job responses/errors. - Closure path: no S3-specific corrective closure established. Eval failures can stop or continue an evaluation scenario and are reported to the evaluator/caller, but the reviewed runtime does not feed a distinct auditor finding back into the audited bot&#39;s current work to require revision/retry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate judge exists, but independence alone is insufficient; the missing production feedback closure prevents classification as A or C S3. - Evidence: scriptdriver.py; simulation.py; openclaw-agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream applications can consume eval failures and implement corrective loops; caller composition is not credited as first-party Pipecat closure without shipped wiring.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop was established that converts environmental distinctions into persistent changes of Pipecat operating capability/policy used by later work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: persistent conversation memory and dynamic current-session flow/context exist, but no reviewed mechanism closes prospective capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mem0 message storage/search; LLM conversation context; flow transitions and role messages; runtime LLM settings updates; session continuation. - Closure path: no S4-specific closure established. Mem0 stores user/assistant messages and retrieves relevant memories into later context; it does not itself decide or persist a changed skill/tool/policy/capability in response to external/future distinctions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory continuity and current-flow reconfiguration can affect future responses, but the reviewed first-party code does not contain the required sensing → prospective adaptation judgment → persistent capability change → reuse loop. - Evidence: memory.py; flows/manager.py; llmcontext.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream agent can use Pipecat tools to build its own learning/adaptation system; that is outside this standalone runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy loop with legitimate ultimate authority and returned closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bots can have durable system instructions, role messages and application-authored rules, but these are configured operating instructions rather than an S5 adjudication loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an S5 witness would require an unresolved identity/ultimate-policy issue to reach a legitimate parent/ultimate authority and the resulting policy decision to return into later operation; no such first-party path was established. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured systeminstruction; Flow rolemessage; tool schemas; application code; provider/session settings. - Closure path: no S5-specific escalation-and-return path established. Pipecat can apply or update instructions supplied by the application, but that is instruction transport/current configuration rather than ownership of identity or ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither static developer-authored identity nor runtime system-instruction mutation by framework/application code establishes a legitimate ultimate-policy decision loop under Profile 0.2.4. - Evidence: flows/manager.py; llmservice.py; uiworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream product owners may govern bot identity out of band; this assessment does not convert arbitrary application configuration into Pipecat S5 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2f9af52d3fefcbb27eb35f8f736f06a8ef93e432/assessments/pipecat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2f9af52d3fefcbb27eb35f8f736f06a8ef93e432\">2f9af52 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/autogen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft AutoGen AgentChat</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/autogen/tree/027ecf0a379bcc1d09956d46d12d44a3ad9cee14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 027ecf0a379bcc1d09956d46d12d44a3ad9cee14\">027ecf0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: stateful agents choose tools/actions and incorporate returned results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: team selection plus Magentic-One&#39;s explicit loop/stall detection and controlled transfer of the next action right suppress uncontrolled repetition/interference among multiple S1 participants. This is more than mere delegation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: MagenticOneOrchestrator owns a whole-team current facts/plan/progress ledger and has authority to assess progress, choose commitments, reset participants and regenerate the plan when the team stalls. Code-execution approval constrains an operational executor action rather than independently owning this whole-team current-control loop, so no separate (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the progress ledger is part of the same operational S3 regulator that directs the team. It is not a distinct complementary audit channel with separate reviewing independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: replanning after current-task stalls is inside-now adaptation. No distinct external/prospective intelligence function shaping future organizational posture is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: participant set, instructions, termination rules and ultimate authority remain application/parent-owned.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/autogen-agentchat.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">data-to-paper</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Technion-Kishony-lab/data-to-paper/tree/81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 81df14c4b9600466e645c3b2b336cc54daa3df3a\">81df14c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform the environment-facing scientific work needed to transform project data and research state into executable analyses and research artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous datasets, generated-code errors, unexpected outputs/files, statistical-analysis requirements, intermediate product state and manuscript construction requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise analysis/code/content actions in response to current project products, execution results and validation/reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: the active first-party performer/code-writing agents in the hypothesis-testing workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: stage runner, CodeRunner, debugger conversers, code extract/check logic, output requirements, product store, data chaining and configured iteration bounds. - Closure path: current scientific products/data -&gt; agent proposes code/content action -&gt; first-party runner executes/materializes it -&gt; output/error/product state returns -&gt; agent revises or advances. - Boundary reachability: these paths are the shipped hypothesis-testing research workflow invoked by the installable application/CLI, not benchmark-only examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime and guardrails execute/check code, but the context-sensitive analysis/content choice and revision remain model-agent decisions. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py, requestcode.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the workflow contains multiple performer/reviewer roles and research stages, but no concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among distinct S1 units is identified and attenuated by a dedicated coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed stage transitions, product passing, paired conversations, deterministic sequencing and rewind/retry utilities. - Closure path: these mechanisms sequence work and transmit review feedback; they do not close a cross-S1 interference-regulation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interacting agents and state transfer are real, but the reviewed paths are production/review dependencies rather than S2-specific conflict attenuation. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous whole-system inside-and-now control function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage completion, retries, rewinds, project parameters and review rounds constrain current work, but no first-party autonomous actor is shown holding a whole-project current view plus broad authority over shared resources/commitments/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: stage flow and retry bounds are primarily encoded in deterministic runner logic and project parameters; local reviewers control product acceptance rather than organization-wide current control. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DataStepRunner/HypothesisTestingStepsRunner, stage enums, product readiness, retry/rewind helpers, project parameters and UI actions. - Closure path: configured stage/retry conditions alter control flow mechanically; no discretionary whole-system S3 owner is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents make local scientific/product decisions, while the broader workflow sequence is framework-defined. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: provide a separate challenge channel that reviews scientific/code/writing products and returns concrete corrective feedback before those products are accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible-but-wrong analysis outputs, code/result problems, weak hypotheses/plans, writing defects and other product claims that ordinary performer generation may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently approve a product or issue constructive concerns that force another performer revision cycle. - Decision owner: the separate reviewer LLM conversation in ReviewDialogDualConverserGPT and review-specific subclasses; in code paths, review agents inspect executed outputs/code products separately from the generating/debugging conversation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate ConversationManager, review termination phrase, max review rounds, product extraction/checks, code-review prompts and rewind utilities. - Closure path: performer produces valid candidate -&gt; reviewer receives the candidate/background -&gt; reviewer approves or supplies concerns -&gt; concerns are appended to the performer conversation with explicit correction request -&gt; performer rewrites/revises -&gt; review repeats. - Boundary reachability: the review dialog base class is reused by goal, planning and manuscript-writing review steps in the shipped hypothesis-testing workflow; code review is also embedded in the standard code-product path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer is a separate agent conversation and owns the semantic approve/challenge judgment; deterministic loop code only routes/enforces that judgment. - Claim being audited: that a current scientific product/code result is adequate/correct enough to become accepted workflow state. - Ordinary reporting path: performer response plus normal generated/executed product state. - Complementary access path: separate reviewer conversation receives the product and relevant background/output context; code review can inspect created output contents. - Independence boundary: reviewer has its own conversation manager/role and does not share the performer&#39;s assistant role; review feedback is generated independently and then returned. - Who acts on findings: the original performer/code-writing agent revises the product in the next cycle. - Evidence: dualconverser.py, requestcode.py, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: use external literature/novelty evidence to develop or revise future research direction and return the selected direction into the current experimental plan, with both autonomous and Copilot parent-governed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: existing literature may make a candidate goal non-novel, external findings may change relevant hypotheses, and a human scientist may judge the proposed future direction inadequate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in Autopilot, research/novelty agents judge literature-derived novelty and generate/refine the goal/hypothesis; in Copilot, human review can replace/supplement reviewer feedback and require revised research products. - Decision owner: autonomous research/reviewer agents in base mode; human scientist in the supported Copilot parent mode for the reviewed future-direction decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: literature-search agents, NoveltyAssessmentReview, ReGoalReviewGPT, stage return to GOAL, human-review application interactions, product storage and plan handoff. - Closure path: external literature -&gt; novelty/goal review -&gt; autonomous or parent judgment -&gt; if inadequate, workflow returns to goal generation/refinement -&gt; resulting goal/hypothesis/testing plan feeds data analysis and later research stages. - Boundary reachability: literature/novelty/goal refinement is part of the shipped hypothesis-testing runner, and README plus HumanReviewType/review interaction code expose Copilot human feedback through the same first-party workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base mode closes without human direction through model-agent novelty/refinement decisions; the separately supported Copilot mode intentionally transfers the relevant review/judgment right to the human and returns that decision into workflow state. - External distinction: retrieved scientific literature and most-similar-paper evidence. - Future / prospective distinction: whether the current research goal/hypothesis is novel/appropriate enough to warrant future experimentation. - Adaptation option generated: revised research goal/hypothesis and downstream hypothesis-testing plan. - Path back into current capability / S3: failed novelty sets regoal and returns to ScientificStage.GOAL; accepted/revised goal then feeds planning, code analysis and manuscript stages. - Evidence: stepsrunner.py, dualconverser.py, README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project parameters, research goals, human feedback and methodological guardrails constrain work, but no identity-level dispute is routed to an ultimate authority and returned as governing organizational policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: startup/project parameters, user research goal, coding guardrails, reviewer prompts and human-review UI. - Closure path: these controls constrain local research behavior directly; no identity/ultimate-policy issue -&gt; authoritative decision -&gt; returned policy loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither autonomous agents nor the Copilot human are shown resolving an identity-level policy issue at this recursion. - Evidence: README.md, stepsrunner.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/data-to-paper.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T19:11:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CowAgent</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zhayujie/CowAgent/tree/7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7c55a61e1bccb82c99d97a4a436f598c079f7c9d\">7c55a61 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/scheduled/delegated objectives into environment-facing tool/skill work and returned outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, model uncertainty, tool results/errors, files/shell/browser/service state, channel messages, and follow-up observations encountered during a trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool/skill action, incorporate returned observations, delegate bounded work when useful, or stop with a final response. - Decision owner: the running CowAgent Agent/model actor inside the first-party AgentStreamExecutor loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChatService, AgentBridge, provider adapters, prompt/workspace construction, tool registry, permissions, session persistence, scheduler integration and context controls. - Closure path: each tool result, delegated result or new model observation returns into the first-party executor context and can change the next autonomous action until completion. - Boundary reachability: the credited executor/bridge/tool path is the normal shipped Web/channel runtime and is instantiated directly by standard CowAgent operation at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code supplies transport, limits and execution, while the model actor owns the discretionary next-action choice on each loop iteration. - Evidence: pinned agent/chat/service.py and bridge/agentbridge.py; README.md corroborates the shipped planning/tool execution boundary. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider/tool availability, permissions and iteration limits constrain the Agent&#39;s operational discretion but do not replace its next-action judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete interference between sibling operational Agents when concurrent delegations target the same persistent relay conversation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent handoffs to one source→target relay session would otherwise interleave turns in the target Agent&#39;s transcript and corrupt the shared conversational state used by later delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize admission to that target relay session so exactly one delegated turn occupies it at a time; timeout rather than permit an overlapping handoff when the lock cannot be acquired within policy. - Decision owner: constructor-owned at the assessed boundary. The first-party S2-specific serialization primitive is complete and operational, but the conflict policy itself is deterministic/developer-selected rather than autonomously chosen by an Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-relay relaylock, stable relay-session derivation, delegation roster/ACL, depth/cycle checks, timeout and synchronous result return. - Closure path: a source Agent&#39;s delegation blocks on the relay lock; after the prior target turn finishes and releases the lock, the next delegated target run executes against a non-interleaved transcript and its result returns to the waiting source Agent, changing that source&#39;s subsequent S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: agentdelegate is a shipped team tool attached to normal team turns through AgentBridge; no downstream coordinator or test-only actor is required for the serialization path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents autonomously decide to delegate and select the teammate/task, but once two handoffs conflict the coordination choice is a fixed first-party lock policy. CowAgent therefore exposes a real S2-specific construction path without an autonomous owner of the conflict-regulation decision. - Evidence: pinned agent/tools/agentdelegate/agentdelegate.py and team-session support in bridge/agentbridge.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: other team features such as speaker selection, shared transcripts and ordinary handoff are not separately credited as S2 merely because they connect Agents. - Distinct S1 units: independently configured live CowAgent Agents with separate profiles/workspaces and first-party execution loops participating in one team conversation. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping delegated turns to the same target relay session can interleave in the target transcript. - Attenuating coordination relation: the first-party per-relay lock queues competing handoffs and allows only one target turn at a time. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the coordinated target result is returned synchronously to the delegating source Agent&#39;s tool loop; timeout/failure likewise returns a result that changes the source Agent&#39;s next decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the lock exists specifically because concurrent sibling handoffs would interfere by interleaving one operational transcript, and it changes admission/timing to suppress that identified disturbance before results feed back to the source Agent.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the currently configured CowAgent installation as a whole by changing which operational Agents are available, which models/capabilities they use, and which live channel instances/teams route work to them. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current mismatch between installed operational capacity and the operator&#39;s desired roster, agent availability, model/capability allocation, shared/own knowledge mode, default ownership and live channel/team bindings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create/update/archive/delete an Agent, make an Agent default, enable/disable it, alter its model/skills/knowledge allocation, or rebind a running channel/team to a different Agent roster. - Decision owner: the authenticated self-hosted operator is the legitimate parent actor in this supported mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentAdminService, roster revision checks, Web authentication, registry/router rebuilding, scheduler reconciliation, targeted live-runtime eviction and hot channel rebinding. - Closure path: the operator submits a first-party Web/API decision; CowAgent persists it, updates the live registry/router or channel binding, starts/stops the affected schedulers and evicts changed Agent instances so subsequent current work is executed under the returned control decision. - Boundary reachability: the authenticated Web console and its /api/agents administration paths are a standard shipped self-hosted operating surface documented as the unified configuration hub, not a development-only control plane. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no standard first-party autonomous Agent is given installation-wide roster/model/capability/channel authority. The decisive current-control choice comes from the authenticated operator; runtime code only validates, persists and enforces it. - Evidence: pinned agent/admin.py, channel/web/api/agents.py, and bridge/agentbridge.py scheduler/runtime reconciliation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this claim is limited to installation-level current capability/resource control. Per-task steering/cancel and permission enforcement alone would not establish S3, and the assessment does not treat every settings edit as an S3 decision. - Whole-system current view: AgentAdminService.snapshot() exposes the installation&#39;s full current Agent roster, default Agent and channel instances; the same authenticated surface operates over that roster rather than one worker-local state. - Current-control decision scope: availability of S1 units, current model/capability allocation, default Agent, channel/team routing and scheduler participation for the running installation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary and sufficiently independent audit function was established at the declared installation boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none established as an S3 witness; ordinary tool feedback, run/event recording and Self-Evolution review do not provide a materially independent route to operational reality for checking production claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S3-specific independent audit judgment/feedback right is supplied. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution/tool events, conversation/run persistence, evolution records/backups, logs and operator inspection surfaces. - Closure path: no independent audit finding→corrective-current-control loop was established; recorded events and evolution changes serve their primary execution/adaptation purposes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no qualifying audit function whose decisive judgment could be assigned to an autonomous Agent. - Evidence: pinned chat/executor event path, conversation storage, Web/admin surfaces, and agent/evolution/ review path. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could add an independent verifier or external monitor, but that composed system would be outside this frozen first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert externally observed conversation patterns and capability failures into prospective adaptations of reusable future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user preferences and recurring work patterns, skill defects discovered in real use, reusable workflows not yet encoded as capabilities, and unfinished work whose recurrence or omission would reduce future fit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after an idle conversation, decide autonomously whether there is a clear durable signal and whether to patch an existing skill, create a new reusable skill, consolidate knowledge/memory, or take no adaptation action. - Decision owner: the isolated first-party Self-Evolution review Agent/model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: idle trigger thresholds, restricted evolution toolset, guarded workspace paths, pre-change backup, snapshot diff/change detection, transactional rollback and evolution records. - Closure path: the review Agent senses conversation-derived external signals, selects and writes an adaptation, the executor commits only real guarded changes, and later normal Agent runs load/use the revised skill/knowledge/persona state; if no clear signal exists it explicitly leaves capability unchanged. - Boundary reachability: Self-Evolution is started by normal AgentBridge initialization, its configuration class defaults enabled, and the standard pinned config-template.json ships selfevolutionenabled: true; the decision actor is therefore reachable in the supported distribution without borrowing a dogfood or contributor agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: trigger timing, backups and write guards are deterministic support, but the model review Agent owns the discretionary judgment of whether a past external interaction predicts future reusable need and which permitted adaptation to make. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/config.py, trigger.py, executor.py, prompts.py, bridge/agentbridge.py, config-template.json and docs/memory/self-evolution.mdx. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: simple memory consolidation alone is not the credited S4 witness. The positive mapping relies on the separately evidenced prospective skill-adaptation path: detecting a workflow likely to be wanted again or a skill defect exposed in use, autonomously generating an adaptation, and returning that change into later capability. - External distinction: the review consumes user conversation and actual skill/task experience from the environment, including repeated preferences, failures and emergent workflows. - Future / prospective distinction: the evolution prompt explicitly asks whether a workflow is reusable and likely to be wanted again, and documentation defines the goal as creating/fixing skills so they can be reused next time and future conversations improve. - Adaptation option generated: patch a defective skill, encode an emergent workflow as a new reusable skill, consolidate durable knowledge/memory, complete suitable unfinished work, or deliberately make no change. - Path back into current capability / S3: committed workspace/skill/knowledge changes are the same first-party artifacts read by subsequent normal Agent operation; operator rollback remains a separate parent intervention path rather than the owner of the autonomous adaptation judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: govern the Agent&#39;s durable identity/personality and highest-level workspace behavioral principles through first-party identity files that are loaded into subsequent system prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: drift between the Agent&#39;s durable identity/behavioral principles and explicit repeated signals about what the assistant should be, plus explicit operator decisions to redefine those identity/policy artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in the autonomous mode, decide whether an explicit repeated identity/personality/style signal is strong enough to justify a bounded AGENT.md identity revision; in the parent mode, authoritatively edit AGENT.md/RULE.md through the authenticated core-file administration surface. - Decision owner: base mode — the isolated Self-Evolution review Agent for the deliberately narrow autonomous identity-evolution decision; parent mode — the authenticated self-hosted operator as ultimate authority over the installation&#39;s core identity/policy files. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolution signal criteria and guarded writes/backups; AgentAdminService core-file revision checks and atomic writes; authenticated Web endpoint; prompt/workspace loader; targeted runtime eviction after operator edits. - Closure path: autonomous mode: repeated identity-level external signal → review-Agent identity judgment → guarded AGENT.md edit → subsequent runtime loads the changed persona. Parent mode: authenticated operator reads/edits a core identity/policy file → atomic persistence and cached-Agent eviction → subsequent Agent instance reloads that file into its system prompt. - Boundary reachability: both modes are shipped supported runtime paths: default-enabled Self-Evolution may edit AGENT.md under its standard guard, while the authenticated Web Agents API exposes core-file read/write and immediately evicts the changed runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base identity change is not a static prompt or automatic parser; the review Agent must judge whether a rare explicit repeated signal warrants changing its own identity artifact. The separate operator mode retains parent authority to set or override the same identity/policy surface, hence A(P) rather than plain A. - Evidence: pinned agent/evolution/prompts.py, executor.py, agent/prompt/workspace.py, agent/prompt/builder.py, agent/admin.py and channel/web/api/agents.py. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary permission settings, tool approvals, memory/preferences and per-task steering are not credited as S5. The autonomous claim is narrowly limited to the explicit first-party AGENT.md identity-evolution path; operator edits establish the separate parent mode. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: AGENT.md is explicitly defined by the shipped templates as the Agent&#39;s identity/personality and is loaded with RULE.md into the system-prompt context; this is materially different from ordinary task or tool policy. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: base (A) — Self-Evolution Agent decides whether the standard rare-signal criterion warrants a durable identity edit; parent (P) — authenticated operator directly owns the authoritative core-file edit and may override/undo Agent evolution. - Return-to-operation path: both modes persist the core file used by prompt construction; operator writes additionally evict the cached runtime, and later Agent initialization reloads the changed identity/policy context.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4/assessments/cowagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a00011ae5e8e9ee62aa713d51f283559f12102d4\">a00011a &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
